{"prompt_hash": "72ec363ff66bc92f46a491fb8ed4d70a8942867cb4d0913e0bda8563cccef52d", "text": "[\n  {\n    \"query_id\": \"q_000069\",\n    \"response_text\": \"Summary\\nChapter 7 and Chapter 13 are the most common forms of personal bankruptcy in the U.S. Chapter 7 is a liquidation process designed to wipe out unsecured debts quickly, while Chapter 13 is a reorganization plan for individuals with regular income to pay back all or part of their debts over time.\\n\\nAssumptions: This overview assumes federal bankruptcy laws apply; state-specific exemptions for property may vary.\\n\\nComparison Table\\nFeature | Chapter 7 | Chapter 13\\nEligibility | Must pass Means Test (income limits) | Must have regular income and fit debt limits\\nPrimary Residence | Risk of loss if equity exceeds exemptions | Usually kept; arrears paid through plan\\nTypical Duration | 3 to 6 months | 3 to 5 years\\nPrimary Goal | Debt discharge via asset liquidation | Debt repayment and asset retention\\n\\nDetailed Differences\\n\\n1. Eligibility Requirements\\n- Chapter 7: Requires a Means Test to ensure your income is below the state median or that you lack the disposable income to pay creditors.\\n- Chapter 13: Open to individuals with a stable income. There are specific debt ceilings (e.g., less than $2.75 million in total debt) that change periodically.\\n\\n2. Treatment of Secured Assets (Primary Residence)\\n- Chapter 7: If you are behind on your mortgage, Chapter 7 does not provide a way to catch up. If you have significant equity above the legal exemption, the trustee may sell the home to pay creditors.\\n- Chapter 13: This is often used to save homes from foreclosure. It allows you to stop foreclosure and pay back the missed mortgage payments (arrears) over the life of the 3 to 5-year plan.\\n\\n3. Process Duration\\n- Chapter 7: A relatively fast process. Once filed, the discharge of debt typically occurs within 90 to 120 days.\\n- Chapter 13: A long-term commitment. You must make monthly payments to a trustee for the duration of the court-approved plan (3 or 5 years) before receiving a discharge.\\n\\nIcons:\\n- Law: https://img.icons8.com/ios/50/law.png\\n- Finance: https://img.icons8.com/ios/50/money-box.png\\n- Time: https://img.icons8.com/ios/50/clock.png\\n\\nQuick Actions:\\n[Button: Find Legal Aid] https://www.lsc.gov/find-legal-aid\\n[Button: U.S. Courts Bankruptcy Basics] https://www.uscourts.gov/services-forms/bankruptcy/bankruptcy-basics\\n\\nSources:\\n- https://www.uscourts.gov/services-forms/bankruptcy\\n- https://www.justice.gov/ust/means-testing\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"query_id\": \"q_000070\",\n    \"response_text\": \"Summary\\nYou are comparing two flight options from Paris to New York with a priority on minimizing travel time due to traveling with a toddler. Option B (Direct) is significantly faster but more expensive than Option A (Layover).\\n\\nAssumptions: Flight durations and prices are based on the figures provided in your query.\\n\\nFlight Comparison\\nOption | Route Type | Total Travel Time | Price | Key Attribute\\nOption A | 1-Stop (Dublin) | 13 Hours | $650 | Lower Cost\\nOption B | Direct | 8 Hours | $920 | Fastest Route\\n\\nTravel Time Calculation\\n- Option A: 13 hours total (includes flight legs and a 4-hour layover).\\n- Option B: 8 hours total (direct flight).\\n- Time Difference: Option B saves 5 hours of total travel time.\\n- Price Difference: Option B costs $270 more than Option A.\\n\\nRecommendation\\nI recommend Option B (Direct Flight). \\nWhen traveling with a toddler, minimizing total travel time and avoiding the logistics of a 4-hour layover (which involves deplaning, waiting in a terminal, and re-boarding) is usually worth the premium. The 5-hour time saving significantly reduces the risk of toddler fatigue and simplifies the journey.\\n\\nLive Verification Framework\\nTo verify current availability and real-time pricing, follow these steps:\\n1. Visit a flight aggregator (e.g., Google Flights or 