s] https://example.com/stew-sides\\n\\nICONS\\n- Slow Cooker: https://example.com/icons/crockpot.png\\n- Meat: https://example.com/icons/beef.png\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"query_id\": \"q_000329\",\n    \"response_text\": \"SUMMARY\\nAuthentic Thai Green Curry starts with a hand-pounded paste to release essential oils, followed by a quick simmer with coconut milk and aromatics.\\n\\nPART 1: GREEN CURRY PASTE (Mortar and Pestle)\\nIngredients:\\n- 10-15 Green Bird's Eye Chilies\\n- 2 tbsp Chopped Shallots\\n- 1 tbsp Chopped Garlic\\n- 1 tbsp Chopped Galangal\\n- 1 tbsp Sliced Lemongrass (white part only)\\n- 1 tsp Sliced Kaffir Lime Peel\\n- 1 tsp Chopped Coriander Root\\n- 1 tsp Toasted Cumin Seeds\\n- 1 tsp Toasted Coriander Seeds\\n- 1 tsp Shrimp Paste (Gapi)\\n- 1/2 tsp Salt\\nProcess: Pound the dry seeds first. Add chilies and salt, pound to a paste. Add fibrous ingredients (lemongrass, galangal, lime peel) one by one, pounding until smooth. Finish with shallots, garlic, and shrimp paste.\\n\\nPART 2: MAIN DISH (Serves 3)\\nIngredients:\\n- 500g Chicken Breast, thinly sliced\\n- 200g Bamboo Shoots (canned/sliced)\\n- 400ml Coconut Milk (Full fat)\\n- 2 tbsp Green Curry Paste\\n- 1 tbsp Palm Sugar\\n- 1.5 tbsp Fish Sauce\\n- 5-6 Thai Basil Leaves\\n- 2 Kaffir Lime Leaves, torn\\n- 1 Red Chili, sliced (for garnish)\\n\\nINSTRUCTIONS\\n1. Fry Paste: Heat 100ml of coconut milk in a wok until it starts to separate (oil beads appear). Add 2 tbsp paste and fry until fragrant.\\n2. Cook Chicken: Add chicken slices. Stir-fry until the outside is no longer raw.\\n3. Simmer: Add the rest of the coconut milk and bamboo shoots. Bring to a gentle simmer.\\n4. Season: Add fish sauce and palm sugar. Adjust to balance salty, sweet, and spicy.\\n5. Finish: Stir in Kaffir lime leaves and Thai basil. Remove from heat immediately.\\n\\nQUICK ACTIONS\\n[Button: Find Thai Ingredients] https://example.com/thai-pantry\\n[Button: Mortar vs Blender Guide] https://example.com/curry-technique\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"query_id\": \"q_000330\",\n    \"response_text\": \"SUMMARY\\nA nutritious, budget-friendly meal for a family of six using pantry staples. This recipe creates a savory black bean stew served over fluffy white rice.\\n\\nASSUMPTIONS\\n- Pantry spices include Salt, Pepper, Cumin, and Garlic Powder.\\n- Cooking oil and water are available.\\n- Prices are based on average US grocery store bulk pricing.\\n\\nCOST ESTIMATE (EXAMPLE)\\n- 500g Black Beans: $1.50\\n- 1kg White Rice: $2.00\\n- 2 Large Onions: $1.50\\n- 1 Can Crushed Tomatoes: $1.50\\n- Total: $6.50 (Well under the $15 budget)\\n\\nINGREDIENTS\\n- 500g Dried Black Beans (soaked overnight)\\n- 1kg White Rice\\n- 2 Large Onions, diced\\n- 1 Can (800g) Crushed Tomatoes\\n- 2 tbsp Oil\\n- 2 tsp Cumin\\n- 1 tsp Garlic Powder\\n- Salt and Pepper to taste\\n\\nINSTRUCTIONS\\n1. Prepare Beans: Drain soaked beans. Place in a large pot with water covering them by 2 inches. Boil, then simmer for 1 to 1.5 hours until tender. Do not drain the liquid yet.\\n2. Sauté: In a separate large pan, sauté diced onions in oil until golden brown.\\n3. Flavor: Stir in crushed tomatoes, cumin, and garlic powder. Simmer for 10 minutes.\\n4. Combine: Add the tomato-onion mixture to the cooked beans. Simmer together for 20 minutes to thicken. Season heavily with salt and pepper.\\n5. Rice: While beans simmer, cook the 1kg of rice (approx. 5 cups dry) in 10 cups of water with a pinch of salt until fluffy.\\n6. Serve: Serve large scoops of beans over the rice.\\n\\nQUICK ACTIONS\\n[Button: More $10 Family Meals] https://example.com/budget-recipes\\n[Button: How to Soak Beans] https://example.com/bean-prep\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"query_id\": \"q_000331\",\n    \"response_text\": \"SUMMARY\\nThese air fryer wings achieve a deep-fried crunch without the oil by using a dry-rub technique and high-convection heat, finished with a sweet and savory glaze.\\n\\nASSUMPTIONS\\n- Wings are separated into flats and drumettes.\\n- Baking powder (not soda) is used to create skin bubbles for extra crunch.\\n\\nINGREDIENTS\\n- 1kg Chicken Wings\\n- 1 tbsp Baking Powder\\n- 1/2 tsp Salt\\n- Glaze: 3 tbsp Honey, 2 tbsp Soy Sauce, 3 cloves Minced Garlic, 1 tsp Ginger\\n\\nINSTRUCTIONS\\n1. Dry the Wings: This is the most important step. Use paper towels to pat the wings until completely bone-dry.\\n2. Coating: Toss wings in a bowl with baking powder and salt until lightly coated.\\n3. Air Fry: Arrange wings in a single layer in the air fryer basket. Do not crowd them.\\n4. Settings: Cook at 400 degrees Fahrenheit (200 Celsius) for 20-25 minutes. Flip the wings halfway through.\\n5. Glaze: While wings cook, simmer glaze ingredients in a small pan for 3 minutes until thickened.\\n6. Toss: Once wings are golden and crispy, transfer to a bowl and toss with the warm glaze.\\n\\nTOTAL COOKING TIME\\n- 25 minutes\\n\\nQUICK ACTIONS\\n[Button: Air Fryer Temp Chart] https://example.com/air-fryer-temps\\n[Button: Best Honey Brands] https://example.com/honey-guide\\n\\nICONS\\n- Crispy: https://example.com/icons/fire.png\\n- Fast: https://example.com/icons/timer.png\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"query_id\": \"q_000332\",\n    \"response_text\": \"SUMMARY\\nA sourdough starter is a living culture of wild yeast and bacteria. This 7-day schedule will help you build a strong, active starter from just flour and water.\\n\\nASSUMPTIONS\\n- Use Whole Wheat or Rye flour for Day 1 to kickstart fermentation.\\n- Use All-Purpose or Bread flour for subsequent days.\\n- Ambient temperature: 70-75 degrees Fahrenheit (21-24 Celsius).\\n\\n7-DAY SCHEDULE\\n- Day 1: Mix 50g flour + 50g water. Let sit 24h.\\n- Day 2: Discard half. Add 50g flour + 50g water. (1:1:1 ratio)\\n- Day 3: Discard half. Add 50g flour + 50g water. Look for small bubbles.\\n- Day 4: Discard half. Add 50g flour + 50g water. Should smell slightly sour.\\n- Day 5: Feed twice (Morning/Evening). Discard half, add 50g flour + 50g water each time.\\n- Day 6: Feed twice. Starter s