{"prompt_hash": "93f81466fd1bf144b0f6bbbcdf4bb480d8a3d41aca1e68a3695f5aebbab72160", "text": "[\n  {\n    \"query_id\": \"q_000401\",\n    \"response_text\": \"Summary\\nWhen a lookup value is text but the source table contains numbers, Excel fails to match them because the data types do not align. You must either convert the lookup value to a number within the formula or convert the source data to text.\\n\\nAssumptions: The lookup value is in cell A2 and the source table is in range D2:E100.\\n\\nFormula-Based Solution\\nUse the VALUE function to convert the text lookup value into a number so it matches the source table.\\n- Formula: =VLOOKUP(VALUE(A2), D2:E100, 2, FALSE)\\n- Alternative (if the source is text and lookup is number): =VLOOKUP(A2&\\\"\\\", D2:E100, 2, FALSE)\\n\\nFormatting-Based Solution\\nUse the Text to Columns feature to physically change the data type of the source column.\\n1. Select the column in your source table containing the numbers.\\n2. Go to the Data tab and select Text to Columns.\\n3. Select Delimited and click Next.\\n4. Uncheck all delimiters and click Next.\\n5. Select General or Number as the Column data format and click Finish.\\n6. Ensure the lookup value cell (A2) is also formatted as a Number.\\n\\nQuick Actions:\\n[Button: Microsoft Excel Help] https://support.microsoft.com/en-us/excel\\n\\nSources:\\n- https://support.microsoft.com/en-us/office/vlookup-function-0bbc8083-26fe-4963-8ab8-93a18ad188a1\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"query_id\": \"q_000402\",\n    \"response_text\": \"Summary\\nThe Linksys EA8300 (Max-Stream AC2200) is managed through a web-based setup page or the Linksys app. Accessing the local interface allows for direct control over Wi-Fi settings.\\n\\nAdministrative Access Info\\n- Default Local IP: 192.168.1.1\\n- Standard Username: admin\\n- Standard Password: admin (or the password you set during initial setup)\\n\\nStep-by-Step Guide\\n1. Connect a device to the router via Ethernet or Wi-Fi.\\n2. Open a web browser and enter 192.168.1.1 in the address bar.\\n3. Log in using the administrative credentials.\\n4. Click on Wireless in the left-hand menu under Router Settings.\\n5. To Change SSID: Locate the Network Name (SSID) field for the 2.4GHz and 5GHz bands. Enter your new desired names.\\n6. To Change Password: Look for the Password field under the Security Mode section (ensure WPA2 Personal is selected). Enter your new password.\\n7. Click Apply or Save at the bottom of the page. The router may reboot, and you will need to reconnect your devices using the new credentials.\\n\\nQuick Actions:\\n[Button: Linksys EA8300 Support] https://www.linksys.com/support-product?sku=EA8300\\n\\nSources:\\n- https://www.linksys.com/support-article?articleNum=216444\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"query_id\": \"q_000403\",\n    \"response_text\": \"Summary\\nThis Python script utilizes the os module to traverse the specified directory and the os.path.getsize function to determine file sizes. It converts bytes to megabytes (MB) for the final output.\\n\\nPython Script\\n\\nimport os\\n\\ndef find_large_files(directory, threshold_mb):\\n    threshold_bytes = threshold_mb * 1024 * 1024\\n    large_files = []\\n    \\n    for root, dirs, files in os.walk(directory):\\n        for name in files:\\n            filepath = os.path.join(root, name)\\n            try:\\n                file_size = os.path.getsize(filepath)\\n                if file_size > threshold_bytes:\\n                    size_mb = round(file_size / (1024 * 1024), 2)\\n                    large_files.append((name, size_mb))\\n            except OSError:\\n                # Skip files that cannot be accessed\\n                continue\\n    return large_files\\n\\npath = '/home/user/documents'\\nresults = find_large_files(path, 100)\\n\\nprint(\\\"Files larger than 100MB:\\\")\\nfor file, size in results:\\n    print(f\\\"{file}: {size} MB\\\")\\n\\nAssumptions: The user has read permissions for the specified directory and its subdirectories.\\n\\nQuick Actions:\\n[Button: Python Documentation] https://docs.python.org/3/\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"query_id\": \"q_000404\",\n    \"response_text\": \"Summary\\nA 'Load Paper' error when the tray is full usually indicates the printer cannot detect the paper or the pickup rollers are failing to grab a sheet. This is common on the HP LaserJet Pro M404n due to dust buildup.\\n\\nTroubleshooting Steps\\n1. Clean the Pickup Rollers: Remove Tray 2 and locate the rubber rollers inside the top of the tray cavity. Wipe them with a lint-free cloth lightly dampened with water to remove paper dust and restore grip.\\n2. Inspect the Paper Width Guides: Ensure the blue plastic guides in Tray 2 are snug against the A4 paper stack. If they are too loose or too tight, the paper sensor may not register the stack correctly.\\n3. Clean the Paper Presence Sensor: Locate the small plastic flag (sensor) near the back of the tray area. Use a can of compressed air to blow away any debris that might be preventing the sensor from moving freely.\\n4. Reset the Separation Pad: Check the rubber pad at the front of Tray 2. If it is worn smooth, it may cause misfeeds. Clean it with a damp cloth or replace it if visible wear is present.\\n5. Perform a Power Cycle: Turn the printer off, unplug the power cord for 60 seconds, and then plug it directly into a wall outlet (avoiding surge protectors) to recalibrate the internal sensors.\\n\\nIcons:\\n- Printer: https://img.icons8.com/ios/50/000000/printer.png\\n- Maintenance: https://img.icons8.com/ios/50/000000/settings.png\\n\\nSources:\\n- https://support.hp.com/us-en/product/hp-laserjet-pro-m403-m404-series/19203822\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"query_id\": \"q_000405\",\n    \"response_text\": \"Summary\\nEncryption is the process of encoding information to prevent unauthorized access. Symmetric and asymmetric methods differ primarily in how they handle keys.\\n\\nComparison Table: Encryption Types\\nFeature | Symmetric Encryption | Asymmetric Encryption\\nKey Usage | Same key for encryption and decryption | Different keys (Public and Private)\\nSpeed | Very Fast | Slower due to complex math\\nSecurity | Riskier key exchange | Secure key exchange\\nCommon Use | Bulk data, Hard drives | Digital signatures, SSL/TLS\\nAlgorithms | AES, DES, ChaCha20 | RSA, ECC, Diffie-Hellman\\n\\n5-Point Wi-Fi Security Checklist\\n1. Enable WPA3 or WPA2-AES encryption (Avoid WEP or WPA).\\n2. Change the default SSID to something unique that doesn't identify you.\\n3. Use a complex admin password for the router interface (not the Wi-Fi password).\\n4. Disable WPS (Wi-Fi Protected Setup) to prevent PIN-guessing attacks.\\n5. Disable Remote Management to ensure the router can only be configured from within your home.\\n\\nQuick Actions:\\n[Button: Learn More about AES] https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Advanced_Encryption_Standard\\n\\nIcons:\\n- Security: https://img.icons8.com/ios/50/000000/shield.png\\n- Wi-Fi: https://img.icons8.com/ios/50/000000/wi-fi.png\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"query_id\": \"q_000406\",\n    \"response_text\": \"Summary\\nTo target a specific process based on CPU usage in PowerShell, you must retrieve the process object, check its properties, and pipe it to the termination command.\\n\\nPowerShell Command\\nGet-Process -Name \\\"SystemTask\\\" | Where-Object { $_.CPU -gt 50 } | Stop-Process -Force\\n\\nCommand Breakdown\\n- Get-Process -Name \\\"SystemTask\\\": This finds all active processes with the name 'SystemTask' (Note: .exe is omitted in the name parameter).\\n- |: The pipe operator takes the output of the previous command and passes it to the next.\\n- Where-Object { $_.CPU -gt 50 }: This filters the list. '$_' represents the current object in the pipe, '.CPU' checks the total processor time used, and '-gt 50' specifies 'greater than 50'.\\n- Stop-Process -Force: This terminates the processes that passed the filter immediately without asking for confirmation.\\n\\nAssumptions: The 'CPU' property in Get-Process represents total processor time in seconds. For real-time percentage monitoring, a more complex script using 'Get-Counter' would be required, but this is the standard approach for basic management.\\n\\nQuick Actions:\\n[Button: PowerShell Documentation] https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/powershell/\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"query_id\": \"q_000407\",\n    \"response_text\": \"Summary\\nResetting NVRAM and SMC can resolve issues related to power, battery, fans, and display on Intel-based Macs. Since the 2019 MacBook Pro contains the Apple T2 Security Chip, the SMC reset procedure is specific.\\n\\nNVRAM Reset Procedure\\n1. Shut down your Mac.\\n2. Press the Power button, then immediately press and hold these four keys: Option + Command + P + R.\\n3. Keep holding for about 20 seconds. You can release them after you see the Apple logo appear and disappear for the second time or after you hear a second startup chime.\\n\\nSMC Reset Procedure (T2 Chip Models)\\n1. Shut down your Mac.\\n2. Press and hold the following keys on your built-in keyboard: Control (Left side) + Option (Left side) + Shift (Right side).\\n3. Keep holding these three keys for 7 seconds, then press and hold the Power button as well.\\n4. Keep holding all four keys for another 7 seconds, then release them.\\n5. Wait a few seconds, then press the Power button to turn on your Mac.\\n\\nIcons:\\n- Mac: https://img.icons8.com/ios/50/000000/macbook.png\\n- Reset: https://img.icons8.com/ios/50/000000/restart.png\\n\\nSources:\\n- https://support.apple.com/en-us/HT204063\\n- https://support.apple.com/en-us/HT201295\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"query_id\": \"q_000408\",\n    \"response_text\": \"Summary\\nThis solution uses a checkbox hack for the mobile menu toggle, allowing it to remain CSS-only without requiring JavaScript.\\n\\nHTML Structure\\n<nav class=\\\"navbar\\\">\\n  <div class=\\\"logo\\\">Logo</div>\\n  <input type=\\\"checkbox\\\" id=\\\"click\\\">\\n  <label for=\\\"click\\\" class=\\\"menu-btn\\\">&#9776;</label>\\n  <ul>\\n    <li><a href=\\\"#\\\">Home</a></li>\\n    <li><a href=\\\"#\\\">Services</a></li>\\n    <li><a href=\\\"#\\\">Portfolio</a></li>\\n    <li><a href=\\\"#\\\">Contact</a></li>\\n  </ul>\\n</nav>\\n\\nCSS Code\\n* { margin: 0; padding: 0; box-sizing: border-box; }\\n.navbar { display: flex; height: 80px; width: 100%; background: #333; align-items: center; justify-content: space-between; padding: 0 50px; color: #fff; }\\n.navbar ul { display: flex; list-style: none; }\\n.navbar ul li { margin: 0 5px; }\\n.navbar ul li a { color: #fff; text-decoration: none; font-size: 18px; padding: 8px 15px; }\\n.menu-btn, #click { display: none; }\\n\\n@media (max-width: 768px) {\\n  .menu-btn { display: block; font-size: 30px; cursor: pointer; }\\n  .navbar ul { position: fixed; top: 80px; left: -100%; background: #111; height: 100vh; width: 100%; display: block; text-align: center; transition: all 0.3s ease; }\\n  #click:checked ~ ul { left: 0; }\\n  .navbar ul li { margin: 40px 0; }\\n  .navbar ul li a { font-size: 20px; }\\n}\\n\\nQuick Actions:\\n[Button: CSS Validator] https://jigsaw.w3.org/css-validator/\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"query_id\": \"q_000409\",\n    \"response_text\": \"Summary\\nTo calculate monthly totals, we use the strftime function (SQLite), DATE_FORMAT (MySQL), or FORMAT (SQL Server) to group by the month part of the date, then aggregate the amounts.\\n\\nSQL Query (Standard MySQL/PostgreSQL approach)\\nSELECT \\n    DATE_FORMAT(OrderDate, '%Y-%m') AS OrderMonth, \\n    SUM(TotalAmount) AS TotalSales\\nFROM \\n    Orders\\nWHERE \\n    OrderDate >= '2023-01-01' AND OrderDate <= '2023-12-31'\\nGROUP BY \\n    OrderMonth\\nORDER BY \\n    OrderMonth ASC;\\n\\nAssumptions: The database is MySQL. If using SQL Server, use FORMAT(OrderDate, 'yyyy-MM'). If using PostgreSQL, use TO_CHAR(OrderDate, 'YYYY-MM').\\n\\nExample Output Table\\nOrderMonth | TotalSales\\n2023-01 | 15200.50\\n2023-02 | 12850.75\\n2023-03 | 18400.00\\n\\nQuick Actions:\\n[Button: SQL Formatter] https://sqlformat.org/\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"query_id\": \"q_000410\",\n    \"response_text\": \"Summary\\nA factory reset of the Philips Hue Bridge is a destructive action used to clear all settings and start fresh. It is typically a last resort for connectivity issues.\\n\\nReset Procedure\\n1. Locate the Philips Hue Bridge (2nd Gen is the square model).\\n2. Keep the Bridge powered on.\\n3. Turn the Bridge over to find the Restore Factory Settings button (a small pinhole on the back).\\n4. Use a paperclip or similar tool to press and hold the button for 5 seconds.\\n5. The LED lights on the front will blink, indicating the reset is in progress.\\n\\nConsequences of Resetting\\n- Connected Bulbs: All bulbs will be disconnected and must be manually re-added. You may need the 6-digit serial number on each bulb if they aren't found automatically.\\n- Scenes and Routines: All custom scenes, timers, and automated routines will be permanently deleted.\\n- Third-Party Integrations: Connections to Apple HomeKit, Google Home, and Amazon Alexa will be broken. You must set up the HomeKit code (found on the bottom of the Bridge) again.\\n- User Accounts: The Bridge will be unlinked from your Philips Hue account.\\n\\nIcons:\\n- Reset: https://img.icons8.com/ios/50/000000/delete-database.png\\n- Smart Home: https://img.icons8.com/ios/50/000000/smart-home.png\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"query_id\": \"q_000411\",\n    \"response_text\": \"Summary\\nThese commands ensure that the web server (typically running as www-data) has the appropriate ownership and permissions to serve files while maintaining security.\\n\\nLinux Terminal Commands\\n1. Change Ownership Recursively:\\nsudo chown -R www-data:www-data /var/www/html\\n\\n2. Set Directory Permissions (755):\\nfind /var/www/html -type d -exec chmod 755 {} +\\n\\n3. Set File Permissions (644):\\nfind /var/www/html -type f -exec chmod 644 {} +\\n\\nExplanation of Permissions\\n- 755 (Directories): Owner can read/write/execute; Group and Others can read/execute. Necessary for navigating folders.\\n- 644 (Files): Owner can read/write; Group and Others can only read. Prevents unauthorized modification of scripts.\\n\\nQuick Actions:\\n[Button: Linux Permissions Guide] https://linuxize.com/post/chmod-command-in-linux/\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"query_id\": \"q_000412\",\n    \"response_text\": \"Summary\\nWhen Outlook is 'Working Offline', it is disconnected from the mail server. This can be caused by a simple setting toggle or an underlying network issue.\\n\\nTroubleshooting Checklist\\n1. Toggle Offline Mode: Go to the Send / Receive tab and check if the Work Offline button is shaded. Click it to toggle back to online.\\n2. Check Internet Connection: Verify your Windows 11 taskbar shows an active internet connection. Open a browser to confirm.\\n3. Restart Outlook: Close the application completely and reopen it to force a reconnection attempt.\\n4. Check for Updates: Go to File > Office Account > Update Options > Update Now to ensure no bugs are causing the disconnect.\\n5. Use 'Repair' Profile: Go to File > Account Settings > Account Settings. Select your account and click Repair.\\n6. Disable Add-ins: Start Outlook in Safe Mode (Hold Ctrl while launching) to see if a third-party add-in is blocking the connection.\\n\\nIcons:\\n- Outlook: https://img.icons8.com/color/48/000000/microsoft-outlook-2019.png\\n- Offline: https://img.icons8.com/ios/50/000000/offline.png\\n\\nSources:\\n- https://support.microsoft.com/en-us/office/switch-from-working-offline-to-working-online-2460e4a8-16c7-47fc-b204-b15492751cff\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"query_id\": \"q_000413\",\n    \"response_text\": \"Summary\\nBattery health is evaluated based on the maximum capacity relative to when it was new and the number of full charge cycles completed. Apple generally recommends service when capacity drops below 80%.\\n\\nDevice Data Analysis\\n- Device A (iPhone 13): 82% Capacity | 450 Cycles\\n- Device B (iPad Pro): 91% Capacity | 120 Cycles\\n\\nService Recommendation\\nDevice A (iPhone 13) is approaching the need for service, while Device B is in excellent health. However, technically, NEITHER device has met the strict 80% replacement threshold yet.\\n\\nDetailed Analysis\\nOption 1: Device A (iPhone 13) | Nearing Threshold | 82% capacity is only 2% away from the service trigger. With 450 cycles, it is close to the 500-cycle benchmark where performance throttling often begins. | Recommendation: Monitor closely; plan for replacement in 3-6 months.\\nOption 2: Device B (iPad Pro) | Healthy | 91% capacity and low cycle count (120) indicate the battery is performing well. iPads are rated for 1000 full cycles before hitting 80% capacity. | Recommendation: No service needed.\\n\\nQuick Actions:\\n[Button: Apple Battery Support] https://www.apple.com/batteries/service-and-recycling/\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"query_id\": \"q_000414\",\n    \"response_text\": \"Summary\\n'Git fetch' downloads changes from the remote repository but does not merge them into your local work. 'Git pull' is a combination of 'git fetch' and 'git merge', immediately updating your local branch.\\n\\nResolving a Merge Conflict in main.py\\n1. Pull the latest changes: \\ngit pull origin main\\n(This will trigger the conflict if main.py has overlapping changes).\\n\\n2. Open main.py: Look for conflict markers like <<<<<<< HEAD, =======, and >>>>>>>.\\n\\n3. Edit the file: Manually choose which code to keep and remove the markers.\\n\\n4. Add the resolved file: \\ngit add main.py\\n\\n5. Commit the resolution: \\ngit commit -m \\\"Resolved merge conflict in main.py\\\"\\n\\n6. Push to remote: \\ngit push origin main\\n\\nQuick Actions:\\n[Button: Git Documentation] https://git-scm.com/doc\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"query_id\": \"q_000415\",\n    \"response_text\": \"Summary\\nTo maintain high edge quality when removing a white background, it is best to use a combination of selection tools and layer masks rather than the eraser tool.\\n\\nStep-by-Step Guide\\n1. Open the file in Photoshop and ensure the logo is on its own layer (not the 'Background' layer).\\n2. Select the 'Magic Wand Tool' (W). Set the Tolerance to approx 10-20 and ensure 'Contiguous' is checked if the logo is a solid shape.\\n3. Click the white area. If there are white parts inside the logo you also want to remove, uncheck 'Contiguous' and click the white again.\\n4. Go to Select > Inverse to select only the logo.\\n5. Click the 'Add Layer Mask' icon at the bottom of the Layers panel. This hides the white non-destructively.\\n6. To clean edges: Double-click the Layer Mask and use 'Select and Mask' to add a tiny bit of 'Smooth' or 'Contract' (1-2px) to remove any white fringe.\\n7. Go to File > Export > Quick Export as PNG or Export As...\\n8. Choose PNG-24 and ensure the 'Transparency' checkbox is ticked.\\n\\nQuick Actions:\\n[Button: Adobe Photoshop Tutorials] https://helpx.adobe.com/photoshop/tutorials.html\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"query_id\": \"q_000416\",\n    \"response_text\": \"Summary\\nGrading systems in Excel can be handled via nested IF statements for compatibility with older versions or the streamlined IFS function for Excel 2019 and Office 365.\\n\\nNested IF Formula (Universal)\\n=IF(A2>=90, \\\"A\\\", IF(A2>=80, \\\"B\\\", IF(A2>=70, \\\"C\\\", IF(A2>=60, \\\"D\\\", \\\"F\\\"))))\\n\\nIFS Function Formula (Recommended for Newer Excel)\\n=IFS(A2>=90, \\\"A\\\", A2>=80, \\\"B\\\", A2>=70, \\\"C\\\", A2>=60, \\\"D\\\", TRUE, \\\"F\\\")\\n\\nLogic Breakdown\\n- The formula checks from highest to lowest score.\\n- Once a condition is met (e.g., 85 >= 80), it stops and returns that value.\\n- In the IFS version, 'TRUE' acts as the 'else' or catch-all for anything below 60.\\n\\nQuick Actions:\\n[Button: Excel IFS Function Help] https://support.microsoft.com/en-us/office/ifs-function-36329a26-37b2-467c-972b-4a39bd951d45\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"query_id\": \"q_000417\",\n    \"response_text\": \"Summary\\nFor an ASUS ROG STRIX B550-F (which uses AMI BIOS), 3 long beeps typically indicate a Basic Memory (RAM) error. This means the system cannot detect or initialize the system memory.\\n\\nTroubleshooting Steps\\n1. Reseat the RAM: Turn off the PC and unplug it. Remove the RAM sticks and firmly re-insert them into the slots until the clips click into place.\\n2. Test Single Sticks: If you have two sticks, try booting with only one stick at a time in the 'DIMM_A2' slot to identify if one stick is faulty.\\n3. Clean the Contacts: Use a soft eraser or a lint-free cloth with 90%+ Isopropyl Alcohol to gently clean the gold contacts on the RAM sticks.\\n4. Check Slot Priority: Ensure you are using the recommended slots (usually slots 2 and 4 for a dual-channel kit on this board).\\n5. Clear CMOS: Remove the CR2032 battery from the motherboard for 30 seconds or short the 'CLRTC' pins to reset BIOS settings to default, which can resolve memory timing conflicts.\\n\\nIcons:\\n- Warning: https://img.icons8.com/ios/50/000000/error.png\\n- RAM: https://img.icons8.com/ios/50/000000/memory-slot.png\\n\\nSources:\\n- https://www.asus.com/support/FAQ/1029959/\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"query_id\": \"q_000418\",\n    \"response_text\": \"Summary\\nDeveloper Options is a hidden menu in Android used for advanced configuration. Once enabled, you can toggle USB Debugging for ADB (Android Debug Bridge) tasks.\\n\\nSteps to Enable Developer Options\\n1. Open Settings.\\n2. Scroll to the bottom and tap About phone.\\n3. Tap Software information.\\n4. Find Build number and tap it seven (7) times rapidly. You will see a toast message saying \\\"Developer mode has been turned on.\\\"\\n\\nSteps to Enable USB Debugging\\n1. Go back to the main Settings menu.\\n2. Tap the new Developer options menu (now visible at the very bottom).\\n3. Scroll down to the Debugging section.\\n4. Toggle the switch for USB Debugging to On.\\n5. Tap OK on the confirmation prompt.\\n\\nIcons:\\n- Settings: https://img.icons8.com/ios/50/000000/settings.png\\n- Developer", "raw": {"candidates": [{"content": {"parts": [{"text": "[\n  {\n    \"query_id\": \"q_000401\",\n    \"response_text\": \"Summary\\nWhen a lookup value is text but the source table contains numbers, Excel fails to match them because the data types do not align. You must either convert the lookup value to a number within the formula or convert the source data to text.\\n\\nAssumptions: The lookup value is in cell A2 and the source table is in range D2:E100.\\n\\nFormula-Based Solution\\nUse the VALUE function to convert the text lookup value into a number so it matches the source table.\\n- Formula: =VLOOKUP(VALUE(A2), D2:E100, 2, FALSE)\\n- Alternative (if the source is text and lookup is number): =VLOOKUP(A2&\\\"\\\", D2:E100, 2, FALSE)\\n\\nFormatting-Based Solution\\nUse the Text to Columns feature to physically change the data type of the source column.\\n1. Select the column in your source table containing the numbers.\\n2. Go to the Data tab and select Text to Columns.\\n3. Select Delimited and click Next.\\n4. Uncheck all delimiters and click Next.\\n5. Select General or Number as the Column data format and click Finish.\\n6. Ensure the lookup value cell (A2) is also formatted as a Number.\\n\\nQuick Actions:\\n[Button: Microsoft Excel Help] https://support.microsoft.com/en-us/excel\\n\\nSources:\\n- https://support.microsoft.com/en-us/office/vlookup-function-0bbc8083-26fe-4963-8ab8-93a18ad188a1\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"query_id\": \"q_000402\",\n    \"response_text\": \"Summary\\nThe Linksys EA8300 (Max-Stream AC2200) is managed through a web-based setup page or the Linksys app. Accessing the local interface allows for direct control over Wi-Fi settings.\\n\\nAdministrative Access Info\\n- Default Local IP: 192.168.1.1\\n- Standard Username: admin\\n- Standard Password: admin (or the password you set during initial setup)\\n\\nStep-by-Step Guide\\n1. Connect a device to the router via Ethernet or Wi-Fi.\\n2. Open a web browser and enter 192.168.1.1 in the address bar.\\n3. Log in using the administrative credentials.\\n4. Click on Wireless in the left-hand menu under Router Settings.\\n5. To Change SSID: Locate the Network Name (SSID) field for the 2.4GHz and 5GHz bands. Enter your new desired names.\\n6. To Change Password: Look for the Password field under the Security Mode section (ensure WPA2 Personal is selected). Enter your new password.\\n7. Click Apply or Save at the bottom of the page. The router may reboot, and you will need to reconnect your devices using the new credentials.\\n\\nQuick Actions:\\n[Button: Linksys EA8300 Support] https://www.linksys.com/support-product?sku=EA8300\\n\\nSources:\\n- https://www.linksys.com/support-article?articleNum=216444\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"query_id\": \"q_000403\",\n    \"response_text\": \"Summary\\nThis Python script utilizes the os module to traverse the specified directory and the os.path.getsize function to determine file sizes. It converts bytes to megabytes (MB) for the final output.\\n\\nPython Script\\n\\nimport os\\n\\ndef find_large_files(directory, threshold_mb):\\n    threshold_bytes = threshold_mb * 1024 * 1024\\n    large_files = []\\n    \\n    for root, dirs, files in os.walk(directory):\\n        for name in files:\\n            filepath = os.path.join(root, name)\\n            try:\\n                file_size = os.path.getsize(filepath)\\n                if file_size > threshold_bytes:\\n                    size_mb = round(file_size / (1024 * 1024), 2)\\n                    large_files.append((name, size_mb))\\n            except OSError:\\n                # Skip files that cannot be accessed\\n                continue\\n    return large_files\\n\\npath = '/home/user/documents'\\nresults = find_large_files(path, 100)\\n\\nprint(\\\"Files larger than 100MB:\\\")\\nfor file, size in results:\\n    print(f\\\"{file}: {size} MB\\\")\\n\\nAssumptions: The user has read permissions for the specified directory and its subdirectories.\\n\\nQuick Actions:\\n[Button: Python Documentation] https://docs.python.org/3/\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"query_id\": \"q_000404\",\n    \"response_text\": \"Summary\\nA 'Load Paper' error when the tray is full usually indicates the printer cannot detect the paper or the pickup rollers are failing to grab a sheet. This is common on the HP LaserJet Pro M404n due to dust buildup.\\n\\nTroubleshooting Steps\\n1. Clean the Pickup Rollers: Remove Tray 2 and locate the rubber rollers inside the top of the tray cavity. Wipe them with a lint-free cloth lightly dampened with water to remove paper dust and restore grip.\\n2. Inspect the Paper Width Guides: Ensure the blue plastic guides in Tray 2 are snug against the A4 paper stack. If they are too loose or too tight, the paper sensor may not register the stack correctly.\\n3. Clean the Paper Presence Sensor: Locate the small plastic flag (sensor) near the back of the tray area. Use a can of compressed air to blow away any debris that might be preventing the sensor from moving freely.\\n4. Reset the Separation Pad: Check the rubber pad at the front of Tray 2. If it is worn smooth, it may cause misfeeds. Clean it with a damp cloth or replace it if visible wear is present.\\n5. Perform a Power Cycle: Turn the printer off, unplug the power cord for 60 seconds, and then plug it directly into a wall outlet (avoiding surge protectors) to recalibrate the internal sensors.\\n\\nIcons:\\n- Printer: https://img.icons8.com/ios/50/000000/printer.png\\n- Maintenance: https://img.icons8.com/ios/50/000000/settings.png\\n\\nSources:\\n- https://support.hp.com/us-en/product/hp-laserjet-pro-m403-m404-series/19203822\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"query_id\": \"q_000405\",\n    \"response_text\": \"Summary\\nEncryption is the process of encoding information to prevent unauthorized access. Symmetric and asymmetric methods differ primarily in how they handle keys.\\n\\nComparison Table: Encryption Types\\nFeature | Symmetric Encryption | Asymmetric Encryption\\nKey Usage | Same key for encryption and decryption | Different keys (Public and Private)\\nSpeed | Very Fast | Slower due to complex math\\nSecurity | Riskier key exchange | Secure key exchange\\nCommon Use | Bulk data, Hard drives | Digital signatures, SSL/TLS\\nAlgorithms | AES, DES, ChaCha20 | RSA, ECC, Diffie-Hellman\\n\\n5-Point Wi-Fi Security Checklist\\n1. Enable WPA3 or WPA2-AES encryption (Avoid WEP or WPA).\\n2. Change the default SSID to something unique that doesn't identify you.\\n3. Use a complex admin password for the router interface (not the Wi-Fi password).\\n4. Disable WPS (Wi-Fi Protected Setup) to prevent PIN-guessing attacks.\\n5. Disable Remote Management to ensure the router can only be configured from within your home.\\n\\nQuick Actions:\\n[Button: Learn More about AES] https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Advanced_Encryption_Standard\\n\\nIcons:\\n- Security: https://img.icons8.com/ios/50/000000/shield.png\\n- Wi-Fi: https://img.icons8.com/ios/50/000000/wi-fi.png\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"query_id\": \"q_000406\",\n    \"response_text\": \"Summary\\nTo target a specific process based on CPU usage in PowerShell, you must retrieve the process object, check its properties, and pipe it to the termination command.\\n\\nPowerShell Command\\nGet-Process -Name \\\"SystemTask\\\" | Where-Object { $_.CPU -gt 50 } | Stop-Process -Force\\n\\nCommand Breakdown\\n- Get-Process -Name \\\"SystemTask\\\": This finds all active processes with the name 'SystemTask' (Note: .exe is omitted in the name parameter).\\n- |: The pipe operator takes the output of the previous command and passes it to the next.\\n- Where-Object { $_.CPU -gt 50 }: This filters the list. '$_' represents the current object in the pipe, '.CPU' checks the total processor time used, and '-gt 50' specifies 'greater than 50'.\\n- Stop-Process -Force: This terminates the processes that passed the filter immediately without asking for confirmation.\\n\\nAssumptions: The 'CPU' property in Get-Process represents total processor time in seconds. For real-time percentage monitoring, a more complex script using 'Get-Counter' would be required, but this is the standard approach for basic management.\\n\\nQuick Actions:\\n[Button: PowerShell Documentation] https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/powershell/\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"query_id\": \"q_000407\",\n    \"response_text\": \"Summary\\nResetting NVRAM and SMC can resolve issues related to power, battery, fans, and display on Intel-based Macs. Since the 2019 MacBook Pro contains the Apple T2 Security Chip, the SMC reset procedure is specific.\\n\\nNVRAM Reset Procedure\\n1. Shut down your Mac.\\n2. Press the Power button, then immediately press and hold these four keys: Option + Command + P + R.\\n3. Keep holding for about 20 seconds. You can release them after you see the Apple logo appear and disappear for the second time or after you hear a second startup chime.\\n\\nSMC Reset Procedure (T2 Chip Models)\\n1. Shut down your Mac.\\n2. Press and hold the following keys on your built-in keyboard: Control (Left side) + Option (Left side) + Shift (Right side).\\n3. Keep holding these three keys for 7 seconds, then press and hold the Power button as well.\\n4. Keep holding all four keys for another 7 seconds, then release them.\\n5. Wait a few seconds, then press the Power button to turn on your Mac.\\n\\nIcons:\\n- Mac: https://img.icons8.com/ios/50/000000/macbook.png\\n- Reset: https://img.icons8.com/ios/50/000000/restart.png\\n\\nSources:\\n- https://support.apple.com/en-us/HT204063\\n- https://support.apple.com/en-us/HT201295\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"query_id\": \"q_000408\",\n    \"response_text\": \"Summary\\nThis solution uses a checkbox hack for the mobile menu toggle, allowing it to remain CSS-only without requiring JavaScript.\\n\\nHTML Structure\\n<nav class=\\\"navbar\\\">\\n  <div class=\\\"logo\\\">Logo</div>\\n  <input type=\\\"checkbox\\\" id=\\\"click\\\">\\n  <label for=\\\"click\\\" class=\\\"menu-btn\\\">&#9776;</label>\\n  <ul>\\n    <li><a href=\\\"#\\\">Home</a></li>\\n    <li><a href=\\\"#\\\">Services</a></li>\\n    <li><a href=\\\"#\\\">Portfolio</a></li>\\n    <li><a href=\\\"#\\\">Contact</a></li>\\n  </ul>\\n</nav>\\n\\nCSS Code\\n* { margin: 0; padding: 0; box-sizing: border-box; }\\n.navbar { display: flex; height: 80px; width: 100%; background: #333; align-items: center; justify-content: space-between; padding: 0 50px; color: #fff; }\\n.navbar ul { display: flex; list-style: none; }\\n.navbar ul li { margin: 0 5px; }\\n.navbar ul li a { color: #fff; text-decoration: none; font-size: 18px; padding: 8px 15px; }\\n.menu-btn, #click { display: none; }\\n\\n@media (max-width: 768px) {\\n  .menu-btn { display: block; font-size: 30px; cursor: pointer; }\\n  .navbar ul { position: fixed; top: 80px; left: -100%; background: #111; height: 100vh; width: 100%; display: block; text-align: center; transition: all 0.3s ease; }\\n  #click:checked ~ ul { left: 0; }\\n  .navbar ul li { margin: 40px 0; }\\n  .navbar ul li a { font-size: 20px; }\\n}\\n\\nQuick Actions:\\n[Button: CSS Validator] https://jigsaw.w3.org/css-validator/\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"query_id\": \"q_000409\",\n    \"response_text\": \"Summary\\nTo calculate monthly totals, we use the strftime function (SQLite), DATE_FORMAT (MySQL), or FORMAT (SQL Server) to group by the month part of the date, then aggregate the amounts.\\n\\nSQL Query (Standard MySQL/PostgreSQL approach)\\nSELECT \\n    DATE_FORMAT(OrderDate, '%Y-%m') AS OrderMonth, \\n    SUM(TotalAmount) AS TotalSales\\nFROM \\n    Orders\\nWHERE \\n    OrderDate >= '2023-01-01' AND OrderDate <= '2023-12-31'\\nGROUP BY \\n    OrderMonth\\nORDER BY \\n    OrderMonth ASC;\\n\\nAssumptions: The database is MySQL. If using SQL Server, use FORMAT(OrderDate, 'yyyy-MM'). If using PostgreSQL, use TO_CHAR(OrderDate, 'YYYY-MM').\\n\\nExample Output Table\\nOrderMonth | TotalSales\\n2023-01 | 15200.50\\n2023-02 | 12850.75\\n2023-03 | 18400.00\\n\\nQuick Actions:\\n[Button: SQL Formatter] https://sqlformat.org/\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"query_id\": \"q_000410\",\n    \"response_text\": \"Summary\\nA factory reset of the Philips Hue Bridge is a destructive action used to clear all settings and start fresh. It is typically a last resort for connectivity issues.\\n\\nReset Procedure\\n1. Locate the Philips Hue Bridge (2nd Gen is the square model).\\n2. Keep the Bridge powered on.\\n3. Turn the Bridge over to find the Restore Factory Settings button (a small pinhole on the back).\\n4. Use a paperclip or similar tool to press and hold the button for 5 seconds.\\n5. The LED lights on the front will blink, indicating the reset is in progress.\\n\\nConsequences of Resetting\\n- Connected Bulbs: All bulbs will be disconnected and must be manually re-added. You may need the 6-digit serial number on each bulb if they aren't found automatically.\\n- Scenes and Routines: All custom scenes, timers, and automated routines will be permanently deleted.\\n- Third-Party Integrations: Connections to Apple HomeKit, Google Home, and Amazon Alexa will be broken. You must set up the HomeKit code (found on the bottom of the Bridge) again.\\n- User Accounts: The Bridge will be unlinked from your Philips Hue account.\\n\\nIcons:\\n- Reset: https://img.icons8.com/ios/50/000000/delete-database.png\\n- Smart Home: https://img.icons8.com/ios/50/000000/smart-home.png\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"query_id\": \"q_000411\",\n    \"response_text\": \"Summary\\nThese commands ensure that the web server (typically running as www-data) has the appropriate ownership and permissions to serve files while maintaining security.\\n\\nLinux Terminal Commands\\n1. Change Ownership Recursively:\\nsudo chown -R www-data:www-data /var/www/html\\n\\n2. Set Directory Permissions (755):\\nfind /var/www/html -type d -exec chmod 755 {} +\\n\\n3. Set File Permissions (644):\\nfind /var/www/html -type f -exec chmod 644 {} +\\n\\nExplanation of Permissions\\n- 755 (Directories): Owner can read/write/execute; Group and Others can read/execute. Necessary for navigating folders.\\n- 644 (Files): Owner can read/write; Group and Others can only read. Prevents unauthorized modification of scripts.\\n\\nQuick Actions:\\n[Button: Linux Permissions Guide] https://linuxize.com/post/chmod-command-in-linux/\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"query_id\": \"q_000412\",\n    \"response_text\": \"Summary\\nWhen Outlook is 'Working Offline', it is disconnected from the mail server. This can be caused by a simple setting toggle or an underlying network issue.\\n\\nTroubleshooting Checklist\\n1. Toggle Offline Mode: Go to the Send / Receive tab and check if the Work Offline button is shaded. Click it to toggle back to online.\\n2. Check Internet Connection: Verify your Windows 11 taskbar shows an active internet connection. Open a browser to confirm.\\n3. Restart Outlook: Close the application completely and reopen it to force a reconnection attempt.\\n4. Check for Updates: Go to File > Office Account > Update Options > Update Now to ensure no bugs are causing the disconnect.\\n5. Use 'Repair' Profile: Go to File > Account Settings > Account Settings. Select your account and click Repair.\\n6. Disable Add-ins: Start Outlook in Safe Mode (Hold Ctrl while launching) to see if a third-party add-in is blocking the connection.\\n\\nIcons:\\n- Outlook: https://img.icons8.com/color/48/000000/microsoft-outlook-2019.png\\n- Offline: https://img.icons8.com/ios/50/000000/offline.png\\n\\nSources:\\n- https://support.microsoft.com/en-us/office/switch-from-working-offline-to-working-online-2460e4a8-16c7-47fc-b204-b15492751cff\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"query_id\": \"q_000413\",\n    \"response_text\": \"Summary\\nBattery health is evaluated based on the maximum capacity relative to when it was new and the number of full charge cycles completed. Apple generally recommends service when capacity drops below 80%.\\n\\nDevice Data Analysis\\n- Device A (iPhone 13): 82% Capacity | 450 Cycles\\n- Device B (iPad Pro): 91% Capacity | 120 Cycles\\n\\nService Recommendation\\nDevice A (iPhone 13) is approaching the need for service, while Device B is in excellent health. However, technically, NEITHER device has met the strict 80% replacement threshold yet.\\n\\nDetailed Analysis\\nOption 1: Device A (iPhone 13) | Nearing Threshold | 82% capacity is only 2% away from the service trigger. With 450 cycles, it is close to the 500-cycle benchmark where performance throttling often begins. | Recommendation: Monitor closely; plan for replacement in 3-6 months.\\nOption 2: Device B (iPad Pro) | Healthy | 91% capacity and low cycle count (120) indicate the battery is performing well. iPads are rated for 1000 full cycles before hitting 80% capacity. | Recommendation: No service needed.\\n\\nQuick Actions:\\n[Button: Apple Battery Support] https://www.apple.com/batteries/service-and-recycling/\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"query_id\": \"q_000414\",\n    \"response_text\": \"Summary\\n'Git fetch' downloads changes from the remote repository but does not merge them into your local work. 'Git pull' is a combination of 'git fetch' and 'git merge', immediately updating your local branch.\\n\\nResolving a Merge Conflict in main.py\\n1. Pull the latest changes: \\ngit pull origin main\\n(This will trigger the conflict if main.py has overlapping changes).\\n\\n2. Open main.py: Look for conflict markers like <<<<<<< HEAD, =======, and >>>>>>>.\\n\\n3. Edit the file: Manually choose which code to keep and remove the markers.\\n\\n4. Add the resolved file: \\ngit add main.py\\n\\n5. Commit the resolution: \\ngit commit -m \\\"Resolved merge conflict in main.py\\\"\\n\\n6. Push to remote: \\ngit push origin main\\n\\nQuick Actions:\\n[Button: Git Documentation] https://git-scm.com/doc\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"query_id\": \"q_000415\",\n    \"response_text\": \"Summary\\nTo maintain high edge quality when removing a white background, it is best to use a combination of selection tools and layer masks rather than the eraser tool.\\n\\nStep-by-Step Guide\\n1. Open the file in Photoshop and ensure the logo is on its own layer (not the 'Background' layer).\\n2. Select the 'Magic Wand Tool' (W). Set the Tolerance to approx 10-20 and ensure 'Contiguous' is checked if the logo is a solid shape.\\n3. Click the white area. If there are white parts inside the logo you also want to remove, uncheck 'Contiguous' and click the white again.\\n4. Go to Select > Inverse to select only the logo.\\n5. Click the 'Add Layer Mask' icon at the bottom of the Layers panel. This hides the white non-destructively.\\n6. To clean edges: Double-click the Layer Mask and use 'Select and Mask' to add a tiny bit of 'Smooth' or 'Contract' (1-2px) to remove any white fringe.\\n7. Go to File > Export > Quick Export as PNG or Export As...\\n8. Choose PNG-24 and ensure the 'Transparency' checkbox is ticked.\\n\\nQuick Actions:\\n[Button: Adobe Photoshop Tutorials] https://helpx.adobe.com/photoshop/tutorials.html\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"query_id\": \"q_000416\",\n    \"response_text\": \"Summary\\nGrading systems in Excel can be handled via nested IF statements for compatibility with older versions or the streamlined IFS function for Excel 2019 and Office 365.\\n\\nNested IF Formula (Universal)\\n=IF(A2>=90, \\\"A\\\", IF(A2>=80, \\\"B\\\", IF(A2>=70, \\\"C\\\", IF(A2>=60, \\\"D\\\", \\\"F\\\"))))\\n\\nIFS Function Formula (Recommended for Newer Excel)\\n=IFS(A2>=90, \\\"A\\\", A2>=80, \\\"B\\\", A2>=70, \\\"C\\\", A2>=60, \\\"D\\\", TRUE, \\\"F\\\")\\n\\nLogic Breakdown\\n- The formula checks from highest to lowest score.\\n- Once a condition is met (e.g., 85 >= 80), it stops and returns that value.\\n- In the IFS version, 'TRUE' acts as the 'else' or catch-all for anything below 60.\\n\\nQuick Actions:\\n[Button: Excel IFS Function Help] https://support.microsoft.com/en-us/office/ifs-function-36329a26-37b2-467c-972b-4a39bd951d45\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"query_id\": \"q_000417\",\n    \"response_text\": \"Summary\\nFor an ASUS ROG STRIX B550-F (which uses AMI BIOS), 3 long beeps typically indicate a Basic Memory (RAM) error. This means the system cannot detect or initialize the system memory.\\n\\nTroubleshooting Steps\\n1. Reseat the RAM: Turn off the PC and unplug it. Remove the RAM sticks and firmly re-insert them into the slots until the clips click into place.\\n2. Test Single Sticks: If you have two sticks, try booting with only one stick at a time in the 'DIMM_A2' slot to identify if one stick is faulty.\\n3. Clean the Contacts: Use a soft eraser or a lint-free cloth with 90%+ Isopropyl Alcohol to gently clean the gold contacts on the RAM sticks.\\n4. Check Slot Priority: Ensure you are using the recommended slots (usually slots 2 and 4 for a dual-channel kit on this board).\\n5. Clear CMOS: Remove the CR2032 battery from the motherboard for 30 seconds or short the 'CLRTC' pins to reset BIOS settings to default, which can resolve memory timing conflicts.\\n\\nIcons:\\n- Warning: https://img.icons8.com/ios/50/000000/error.png\\n- RAM: https://img.icons8.com/ios/50/000000/memory-slot.png\\n\\nSources:\\n- https://www.asus.com/support/FAQ/1029959/\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"query_id\": \"q_000418\",\n    \"response_text\": \"Summary\\nDeveloper Options is a hidden menu in Android used for advanced configuration. Once enabled, you can toggle USB Debugging for ADB (Android Debug Bridge) tasks.\\n\\nSteps to Enable Developer Options\\n1. Open Settings.\\n2. Scroll to the bottom and tap About phone.\\n3. Tap Software information.\\n4. Find Build number and tap it seven (7) times rapidly. You will see a toast message saying \\\"Developer mode has been turned on.\\\"\\n\\nSteps to Enable USB Debugging\\n1. Go back to the main Settings menu.\\n2. Tap the new Developer options menu (now visible at the very bottom).\\n3. Scroll down to the Debugging section.\\n4. Toggle the switch for USB Debugging to On.\\n5. Tap OK on the confirmation prompt.\\n\\nIcons:\\n- Settings: https://img.icons8.com/ios/50/000000/settings.png\\n- Dev