m-up (5 Minutes)\\n- Activity: 'The Domino Effect' Discussion.\\n- Prompt: Ask students to think of a time a small argument between two people turned into a huge group conflict. Briefly discuss how alliances work in real life.\\n\\nII. Lecture Outline: The M.A.I.N. Causes (20 Minutes)\\n- Militarism: The glorification of military power and the arms race in Europe (e.g., naval rivalry between Britain and Germany).\\n- Alliances: The web of secret treaties (Triple Entente vs. Triple Alliance) that meant one small spark would involve everyone.\\n- Imperialism: Competition for colonies and resources in Africa and Asia leading to diplomatic tension.\\n- Nationalism: Intense patriotism and the desire for self-determination (focus on the Balkans and 'The Powder Keg of Europe').\\n- The Spark: Brief mention of the assassination of Archduke Franz Ferdinand.\\n\\nIII. Primary Source Analysis (25 Minutes)\\n- Activity: Analyzing the 'Blank Check' and Alliance Documents.\\n- Task: Students work in pairs to read excerpts from the Austro-Hungarian ultimatum to Serbia and the German 'Blank Check' telegram. \\n- Questions: How did these documents escalate the situation? Who was being aggressive and who was being defensive?\\n\\nIV. Wrap-up Discussion (10 Minutes)\\n- Question: Which of the M.A.I.N. factors do you believe was the most significant in making the war inevitable? Why?\\n- Exit Ticket: Write down one way the world might look today if the Triple Alliance had won.\\n\\nQuick Actions:\\n- [Button: Download Primary Sources] https://www.archives.gov/education/lessons/world-war-i\\n\\nSources:\\n- https://www.history.com/topics/world-war-i/world-war-i-history\\n- https://learning.blogs.nytimes.com/2014/06/27/the-causes-of-world-war-i/\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"query_id\": \"q_000726\",\n    \"response_text\": \"Comparison of Montessori and Waldorf Educational Philosophies\\n\\nEducational Philosophy Comparison Table\\n\\nCriterion | Montessori | Waldorf\\nClassroom Environment | Prepared, orderly, minimalist, and child-sized. | Home-like, warm, aesthetic, and features natural materials.\\nRole of the Teacher | An observer and guide who facilitates independent work. | A leader and storyteller who stays with the same group for years.\\nUse of Technology | Generally discouraged in early years; focus on tactile tools. | Strictly discouraged in early childhood; focus on imagination.\\nCurriculum Focus | Academics (math/lang) through self-directed 'work' cycles. | Holistic (arts/spirit/physical) through creative 'play'.\\nAge Grouping | Multi-age classrooms (e.g., 3-6 years old together). | Single-age classrooms (peers of the same age).\\n\\nKey Differences Summary:\\nMontessori emphasizes independence, scientific observation, and individual academic mastery. Waldorf emphasizes the collective, imagination, and the developmental stages of the child's spirit and creativity.\\n\\nSources:\\n- https://www.amshq.org/\\n- https://www.waldorfeducation.org/\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"query_id\": \"q_000727\",\n    \"response_text\": \"GRE Vocabulary: Criticism and Praise\\n\\nWords of Criticism:\\n1. Castigate (verb): To reprimand severely.\\n   Example: The director castigated the lead actor for arriving late to every rehearsal.\\n2. Invective (noun): Insulting, abusive, or highly critical language.\\n   Example: The politician's speech was filled with invective against his opponents.\\n3. Vituperation (noun): Bitter and abusive language.\\n   Example: No amount of vituperation from the critics could dampen the artist's spirits.\\n4. Opprobrium (noun): Harsh criticism or censure.\\n   Example: The company faced public opprobrium after the environmental scandal was revealed.\\n5. Diatribe (noun): A forceful and bitter verbal attack against someone or something.\\n   Example: The journalist launched a lengthy diatribe against the new tax policy.\\n6. Berate (verb): To scold or criticize someone angrily.\\n   Example: The coach began to berate the players for their 