f Action and Reaction\\n- Formal Definition: For every action, there is an equal and opposite reaction.\\n- Plain English: Whenever you push something, it pushes back on you just as hard in the opposite direction.\\n- Example: When you kick a soccer ball, the ball flies forward, but your foot feels the force of the ball pushing back against it.\\n\\nIcons:\\n- Physics: https://img.icons8.com/ios/50/physics.png\\n- Motion: https://img.icons8.com/ios/50/fast-forward.png\\n\\nSources:\\n- https://www.nasa.gov/stem-ed-resources/newtons-laws-of-motion.html\\n- https://www.britannica.com/science/Newtons-laws-of-motion\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"query_id\": \"q_000730\",\n    \"response_text\": \"Plot Summary of 'Animal Farm'\\n\\nParagraph 1: The story begins on Manor Farm, where the animals, led by Old Major the pig, revolt against their neglectful human farmer, Mr. Jones. They establish 'Animalism,' a philosophy of equality, and rename the farm 'Animal Farm.' Initially, the animals thrive, working together and following the Seven Commandments, with the most important being 'All animals are equal.'\\n\\nParagraph 2: Power struggles soon emerge between two pigs, Napoleon and Snowball. Napoleon eventually uses a pack of fierce dogs to drive Snowball away and takes total control. Under Napoleon's leadership, the farm becomes increasingly oppressive. The pigs begin to take on human characteristics, living in the farmhouse, drinking alcohol, and changing the commandments to justify their special privileges.\\n\\nParagraph 3: As years pass, the pigs become indistinguishable from the humans they once overthrew. The original dream of equality is completely dead, replaced by the final, single commandment: 'All animals are equal, but some animals are more equal than others.' The animals realize that their new masters are just as tyrannical as the old ones.\\n\\nHistorical Figures and Groups\\n\\n1. Old Major represents Karl Marx and Vladimir Lenin. He provides the original revolutionary 