{"prompt_hash": "d9706a8845eeb4e036c6d8c46c847ee8fb792f59711b595a91e11e73640c8883", "text": "[\n  {\n    \"query_id\": \"q_000721\",\n    \"response_text\": \"Assumptions: This schedule assumes a focus on the Digital SAT format and utilizes 13 total hours of study per week.\\n\\n4-Week SAT Math Study Schedule\\n\\nWeek 1: Heart of Algebra\\n- Monday: Linear equations in one variable (solving and interpreting).\\n- Tuesday: Linear equations in two variables (graphing and slopes).\\n- Wednesday: Linear functions and modeling real-world scenarios.\\n- Thursday: Systems of linear equations (substitution and elimination).\\n- Friday: Linear inequalities in one or two variables.\\n- Saturday (3 Hours): Comprehensive review of Week 1 topics + 20-question practice set on Heart of Algebra.\\n\\nWeek 2: Problem Solving and Data Analysis\\n- Monday: Ratios, rates, and proportions.\\n- Tuesday: Percentages and units of measurement.\\n- Wednesday: One-variable data: distributions, mean, median, and mode.\\n- Thursday: Two-variable data: scatterplots and linear/exponential models.\\n- Friday: Probability and conditional probability.\\n- Saturday (3 Hours): Data analysis deep dive + full practice section focusing on tables/graphs.\\n\\nWeek 3: Passport to Advanced Math\\n- Monday: Operations with polynomials and factoring.\\n- Tuesday: Quadratic equations and functions (vertex and standard form).\\n- Wednesday: Exponential functions and radicals.\\n- Thursday: Rational expressions and equations.\\n- Friday: Function notation and transformations.\\n- Saturday (3 Hours): Advanced math problem set + review of complex quadratic word problems.\\n- Icons:\\n  - Algebra: https://img.icons8.com/ios/50/formula.png\\n  - Data: https://img.icons8.com/ios/50/combo-chart.png\\n\\nWeek 4: Geometry, Trigonometry, and Final Review\\n- Monday: Area and volume formulas.\\n- Tuesday: Lines, angles, and triangles (congruence and similarity).\\n- Wednesday: Right triangle trigonometry and the unit circle.\\n- Thursday: Circles (theorems and equations).\\n- Friday: Full review of weak areas identified in weeks 1-3.\\n- Saturday (3 Hours): Full-length SAT Math practice test under timed conditions + detailed error analysis.\\n\\nQuick Actions:\\n[Button: Practice on Khan Academy] https://www.khanacademy.org/sat\\n[Button: Official College Board Practice] https://satsuite.collegeboard.org/digital/digital-practice-questions/math\\n\\nSources:\\n- https://satsuite.collegeboard.org/sat/whats-on-the-test/math\\n- https://www.khanacademy.org/test-prep/sat-math\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"query_id\": \"q_000722\",\n    \"response_text\": \"Spanish Verb Conjugation: escribir (to write)\\n\\nConjugation Table\\n\\nPronoun | Present | Preterite | Imperfect | Future | Conditional\\nyo | escribo | escribí | escribía | escribiré | escribiría\\ntú | escribes | escribiste | escribías | escribirás | escribirías\\nél/ella/usted | escribe | escribió | escribía | escribirá | escribiría\\nnosotros | escribimos | escribimos | escribíamos | escribiremos | escribiríamos\\nvosotros | escribís | escribisteis | escribíais | escribiréis | escribiríais\\nellos/ellas/ustedes | escriben | escribieron | escribían | escribirán | escribirían\\n\\nQuick Actions:\\n[Button: Practice Spanish Verbs] https://www.spanishdict.com/conjugate/escribir\\n\\nSources:\\n- https://www.rae.es/\\n- https://www.spanishdict.com/\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"query_id\": \"q_000723\",\n    \"response_text\": \"Summary: Photosynthesis is the magical way plants make their own food! Instead of going to the grocery store, plants use sunlight, water, and air to create energy so they can grow big and strong.\\n\\nHow It Works:\\n1. Sunlight: The sun provides the energy. Think of it as the oven that cooks the food.\\n2. Chlorophyll: This is the green stuff inside leaves. It acts like a solar panel, catching the sunlight.\\n3. Water: Plants drink water through their roots. This is a key ingredient for their recipe.\\n4. Carbon Dioxide: This is a gas in the air that we breathe out. Plants breathe it in.\\n5. Glucose and Oxygen: After the plant 'cooks' the ingredients with sunlight, it makes Glucose (a type of sugar/food for the plant) and releases Oxygen into the air for us to breathe.\\n\\n5-Step Summary:\\n- Step 1: The plant drinks water through its roots.\\n- Step 2: The leaves take in carbon dioxide from the air.\\n- Step 3: Chlorophyll in the leaves catches energy from sunlight.\\n- Step 4: The sun's energy turns the water and carbon dioxide into sugar (glucose).\\n- Step 5: The plant keeps the sugar for food and lets out oxygen as a gift for us.\\n\\nImages:\\n- Photosynthesis Diagram: https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/db/Photosynthesis_en.svg/500px-Photosynthesis_en.svg.png\\n\\nSources:\\n- https://www.nasa.gov/audience/forstudents/k-4/dictionary/Photosynthesis.html\\n- https://kids.britannica.com/kids/article/photosynthesis/353617\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"query_id\": \"q_000724\",\n    \"response_text\": \"Solution for 3x^2 - 11x - 4 = 0 using the Quadratic Formula\\n\\nStep 1: Identify the coefficients\\na = 3\\nb = -11\\nc = -4\\n\\nStep 2: Calculate the discriminant (D)\\nThe formula is D = b^2 - 4ac.\\nD = (-11)^2 - 4(3)(-4)\\nD = 121 - (-48)\\nD = 121 + 48\\nD = 169\\n\\nStep 3: Apply the Quadratic Formula\\nx = [-b +/- sqrt(D)] / 2a\\nx = [-(-11) +/- sqrt(169)] / 2(3)\\nx = [11 +/- 13] / 6\\n\\nStep 4: Solve for both values of x\\nCalculation for x1:\\nx1 = (11 + 13) / 6\\nx1 = 24 / 6\\nx1 = 4\\n\\nCalculation for x2:\\nx2 = (11 - 13) / 6\\nx2 = -2 / 6\\nx2 = -1/3 (or approximately -0.33)\\n\\nFinal Values:\\nx = 4, x = -1/3\\n\\nSources:\\n- https://www.khanacademy.org/math/algebra/x2f8bb11595b61c86:quadratic-functions-equations/x2f8bb11595b61c86:quadratic-formula/a/quadratic-formula-review\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"query_id\": \"q_000725\",\n    \"response_text\": \"Lesson Plan: The Causes of World War I\\n\\nDuration: 60 Minutes\\nGrade Level: High School History\\n\\nI. War