ll bank transaction fee of 2.5%).\\n\\nOfficial Website:\\nhttps://indianvisaonline.gov.in/evisa/tvoa.html\\n\\nQuick Actions:\\n[Button: Start e-Visa Application] https://indianvisaonline.gov.in/evisa/tvoa.html\\n\\nIcons:\\n- Passport: https://img.icons8.com/color/48/000000/passport.png\\n- Payment: https://img.icons8.com/color/48/000000/credit-card.png\\n- Success: https://img.icons8.com/color/48/000000/checked-checkbox.png\\n\\nSources:\\n- https://indianvisaonline.gov.in/\\n- https://travel.state.gov/content/travel/en/international-travel/International-Travel-Country-Information-Pages/India.html\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"query_id\": \"q_000649\",\n    \"response_text\": \"Summary: This 7-day road trip follows the rugged Pacific Coast Highway (Hwy 101) from San Francisco to Seattle, featuring ancient forests and dramatic coastlines.\\n\\n7-Day Road Trip Itinerary:\\n\\nDay 1: San Francisco to Mendocino\\n- Nature Stop: Muir Woods National Monument (Redwood forest).\\n- Overnight: Mendocino, CA\\n\\nDay 2: Mendocino to Eureka\\n- Nature Stop: Avenue of the Giants (Humboldt Redwoods State Park).\\n- Overnight: Eureka, CA\\n\\nDay 3: Eureka to Bandon\\n- Nature Stop: Redwood National Park (Lady Bird Johnson Grove).\\n- Overnight: Bandon, OR\\n\\nDay 4: Bandon to Newport\\n- Nature Stop: Oregon Dunes National Recreation Area.\\n- Overnight: Newport, OR\\n\\nDay 5: Newport to Astoria\\n- Nature Stop: Cannon Beach (Haystack Rock).\\n- Overnight: Astoria, OR\\n\\nDay 6: Astoria to Lake Quinault\\n- Nature Stop: Olympic National Park (Ruby Beach).\\n- Overnight: Lake Quinault, WA\\n\\nDay 7: Lake Quinault to Seattle\\n- Nature Stop: Hoh Rain Forest (Hall of Mosses).\\n- Overnight: Seattle, WA\\n\\nRecommended Stops (Cards):\\nOption 1: Muir Woods | Ancient coastal redwoods near SF | $15 entry | https://www.nps.gov/muwo\\nOption 2: Cannon Beach | Iconic Haystack Rock and tide pools | Free | https://www.cannonbeach.org\\nOption 3: Hoh Rain Forest | One of the largest temperate rainforests in the US | $30/car | https://www.nps.gov/olym\\n\\nQuick Actions:\\n[Button: Reserve Muir Woods Parking] https://gomuirwoods.com\\n[Button: National Park Passes] https://www.nps.gov/planyourvisit/passes.htm\\n\\nSources:\\n- https://www.visitcalifornia.com\\n- https://traveloregon.com\\n- https://visitseattle.org\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"query_id\": \"q_000650\",\n    \"response_text\": \"Summary: Capturing the Northern Lights with a beginner DSLR and kit lens (typically 18-55mm) requires manual control and a stable setup to allow enough light into the sensor.\\n\\nEssential Camera Settings (Manual Mode):\\n\\nSetting | Recommended Value | Reasoning\\n--- | --- | ---\\nISO | 1600 - 3200 | Increases sensor sensitivity to light in the dark.\\nAperture | f/3.5 (or lowest available) | Opens the lens as wide as possible to let light in.\\nShutter Speed | 8 - 15 Seconds | Allows time for the faint aurora light to expose the sensor.\\nFocus | Manual (Set to Infinity) | Autofocus will fail in the dark; use 'Live View' to focus on a bright star.\\nWhite Balance | 3500K - 4000K (Incandescent) | Prevents the sky from looking too orange/brown.\\n\\nGear Recommendations:\\n- Tripod: Absolutely essential. Any movement during the long exposure will blur the photo.\\n- Spare Batteries: Cold weather drains batteries very quickly. Keep spares in an inner pocket near your body heat.\\n- Remote Shutter or Self-Timer: Use a 2-second timer to avoid 'camera shake' caused by pressing the button.\\n- Lens Cloth: To wipe away condensation or frost that forms on the glass.\\n\\nPhotography Tips for Beginners:\\n- Remove Filters: Take off UV or polarizing filters to prevent 'rings' (Newton's rings) in the image.\\n- Shoot in RAW: This allows you to adjust the exposure and colors more effectively in editing.\\n- Turn off Image Stabilization: When on a tripod, stabilization can actually cause blur.\\n\\nIcons:\\n- Camera: https://img.icons8.com/color/48/000000/slr-back-side.png\\n- Night: https://img.icons8.com/color/48/000000/bright-moon.png\\n- Tripod: https://img.icons8.com/color/48/000000/tripod.png\\n\\nSources:\\n- https://www.visitnorway.com/things-to-do/nature-highlights/northern-lights/how-to-photograph-the-northern-lights/\\n- https://digital-photography-school.com/step-by-step-guide-to-photographing-the-northern-lights/\"\n  },\n  {\n", "raw": {"candidates": [{"content": {"parts": [{"text": "[\n  {\n    \"query_id\": \"q_000641\",\n    \"response_text\": \"Summary: This 5-day itinerary focuses on the vibrant anime culture, cutting-edge technology, and family-friendly museums of Tokyo, specifically tailored for children aged 8 and 10.\\n\\nAssumptions: Family is staying in a central location like Shinjuku or Shibuya. Travel is via the Tokyo subway system.\\n\\n5-Day Itinerary:\\n\\nDay 1: Akihabara (The Electric Town)\\n- Morning: Explore the multi-story Radio Kaikan for anime merchandise.\\n- Afternoon: Visit a themed cafe (e.g., Square Enix Cafe) and the Gigo Arcade.\\n- Evening: Experience the neon lights and interactive electronics stores.\\n- Neighborhood: Akihabara\\n\\nDay 2: Odaiba (Tech and Fun)\\n- Morning: Miraikan (National Museum of Emerging Science and Innovation) for robotics.\\n- Afternoon: teamLab Planets for an immersive digital art experience.\\n- Evening: See the life-sized Unicorn Gundam Statue.\\n- Neighborhood: Odaiba\\n\\nDay 3: Mitaka and Kichijoji (Studio Ghibli)\\n- Morning: Ghibli Museum (requires booking months in advance).\\n- Afternoon: Inokashira Park and the Ghibli-themed shops.\\n- Evening: Explore the kid-friendly atmosphere of Kichijoji.\\n- Neighborhood: Mitaka / Kichijoji\\n\\nDay 4: Ueno (Culture and Science)\\n- Morning: National Museum of Nature and Science (interactive dinosaur and tech exhibits).\\n- Afternoon: Ueno Zoo or the Tokyo National Museum.\\n- Evening: Ameya Yokocho market for snacks.\\n- Neighborhood: Ueno\\n\\nDay 5: Shibuya and Harajuku (Modern Trends)\\n- Morning: Nintendo TOKYO and