g.icons8.com/ios-filled/50/network.png\\n- Settings: https://img.icons8.com/ios-filled/50/settings.png\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"query_id\": \"q_000426\",\n    \"response_text\": \"Summary: This Dockerfile creates a lightweight, production-ready container for a Node.js application using the Alpine Linux distribution.\\n\\nDockerfile Content:\\n\\n# Use the specific base image\\nFROM node:18-alpine\\n\\n# Set the working directory inside the container\\nWORKDIR /app\\n\\n# Copy package files first to leverage Docker cache for dependencies\\nCOPY package*.json ./\\n\\n# Install production dependencies\\nRUN npm install --production\\n\\n# Copy the rest of the application code\\nCOPY . .\\n\\n# Expose the port the app runs on\\nEXPOSE 3000\\n\\n# Command to run the application\\nCMD [\\\"node\\\", \\\"index.js\\\"]\\n\\nInstructions:\\n1. Save the code above as a file named Dockerfile in your project root.\\n2. Build the image: docker build -t my-node-app .\\n3. Run the container: docker run -p 3000:3000 my-node-app\\n\\nSources:\\n- https://docs.docker.com/language/nodejs/containerize/\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"query_id\": \"q_000427\",\n    \"response_text\": \"Summary: Power Query is a powerful data transformation tool in Excel that can handle large datasets like your 10,000-row CSV efficiently.\\n\\nSteps to Clean Your Data:\\n1. Import Data: Go to the Data tab in Excel, click Get Data > From File > From Text/CSV. Select your file and click Import.\\n2. Open Editor: In the preview window, click Transform Data to open the Power Query Editor.\\n3. Remove Duplicates: \\n   - Locate the CustomerID column.\\n   - Right-click the column header.\\n   - Select Remove Duplicates. This ensures every CustomerID is unique.\\n4. Filter Purchase Amount:\\n   - Locate the PurchaseAmount column.\\n   - Click the drop-down arrow in the column header.\\n   - Select Number Filters > Greater Than.\\n   - Enter 500 in the box and click OK.\\n5. Load Data: Click Close & Load in the top left corner to return the cleaned data to a new Excel worksheet.\\n\\nQuick Actions:\\n[Button: Power Query Documentation] https://support.microsoft.com/en-us/office/about-power-query-in-excel-71047ec7-9512-42b7-af3d-272514c97444\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"query_id\": \"q_000428\",\n    \"response_text\": \"Summary: Adding an M.2 SSD to your PS5 significantly increases storage capacity for PS5 and PS4 games.\\n\\nSSD Requirements:\\n- Interface: PCIe Gen4 x4 M.2 NVMe SSD\\n- Capacity: 250GB - 8TB\\n- Read Speed: 5,500MB/s or faster recommended\\n- Form Factor: 2230, 2242, 2260, 2280, or 22110\\n- Heatsink: Required (either built-in or added manually)\\n- Max Width: 25mm\\n\\nTools Needed:\\n- A #1 Phillips head screwdriver\\n- A flat, stable work surface with a soft cloth\\n\\nInstallation Steps:\\n1. Power off the PS5, unplug all cables, and let it cool down.\\n2. Remove the base. Place the console with the PS logo facing down and the power button facing away from you.\\n3. Gently lift the back corner of the cover and slide it toward the base to remove it.\\n4. Remove the screw from the expansion slot cover and remove the cover.\\n5. Remove the screw and spacer from the 110 mark. Move the spacer to the mark corresponding to your SSD size (usually 80).\\n6. Insert the SSD into the connector at an angle until it clicks, then secure it with the screw over the spacer.\\n7. Reattach the expansion slot cover and the PS5 outer cover.\\n8. Reconnect cables and power on.\\n\\nFormatting:\\n- Upon boot, a M.2 SSD Storage guide will appear. Select Format M.2 SSD. The console will run a speed test and confirm the drive is ready for use.\\n\\nIcons:\\n- Hardware: https://img.icons8.com/ios-filled/50/memory-slot.png\\n- Gaming: https://img.icons8.com/ios-filled/50/playstation.png\\n\\nSources:\\n- https://www.playstation.com/en-us/support/hardware/ps5-install-m2-ssd/\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"query_id\": \"q_000429\",\n    \"response_text\": \"Summary: Choosing a VPN protocol involves balancing speed, security, and device efficiency. WireGuard is currently the industry leader for modern performance.\\n\\nTechnical Comparison:\\n\\nProtocol | Encryption Strength | Connection Speed | Battery Consumption\\nWireGuard | ChaCha20 (Modern/Fast) | Very High | Low (Most Efficient)\\nOpenVPN | AES-256 (Industry Std) | Moderate/High | High (Heavy Load)\\nIKEv2 | AES-256 (Robust) | High | Moderate (Mobile Optimized)\\n\\nKey Insights:\\n- WireGuard: Best for almost all use cases. It has a much smaller code base (approx. 4,000 lines), making it faster to audit and extremely quick to connect.\\n- OpenVPN: Best for compatibility and bypassing strict firewalls (TCP mode). It is highly configurable but can be slower on mobile devices.\\n- IKEv2: Best for mobile users who frequently switch between Wi-Fi and Cellular data, as it excels at re-establishing lost connections quickly.\\n\\nIcons:\\n- Security: https://img.icons8.com/ios-filled/50/shield.png\\n- Speed: https://img.icons8.com/ios-filled/50/speed.png\\n- Battery: https://img.icons8.com/ios-filled/50/battery.png\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"query_id\": \"q_000430\",\n    \"response_text\": \"Summary: The 'Error Establishing a Database Connection' typically means WordPress cannot communicate with your MySQL database due to incorrect credentials or a server failure.\\n\\nTroubleshooting Flowchart:\\n\\n1. Verify wp-config.php Credentials\\n   - Access your site via FTP/SFTP or File Manager.\\n   - Open wp-config.php and check these four lines:\\n     DB_NAME (Database Name)\\n     DB_USER (Username)\\n     DB_PASSWORD (Password)\\n     DB_HOST (Usually 'localhost', but sometimes an IP provided by the host)\\n\\n2. Test Database Connection with a Script\\n   - Create a file named testdb.php in your root directory with a simple PHP mysqli_connect script. If it fails, the credentials in Step 1 are definitely wrong.\\n\\n3. Check MySQL Service Status\\n   - If you have access to the server (VPS/Dedicated), run: service mysql status or service mariadb status.\\n   - If it is stopped, run: sudo service mysql start.\\n\\n4. Repair the Database\\n   - Add define('WP_ALLOW_REPAIR', true); to your wp-config.php file.\\n   - Visit: yoursite.com/wp-admin/maint/repair.php.\\n\\n5. Contact Hosting Provider\\n   - If steps 1-4 fail, the database server may be down or your account may have hit its resource limits.\\n\\nQuick Actions:\\n[Button: WordPress Debugging Guide] https://wordpress.org/documentation/article/debugging-in-wordpress/\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"query_id\": \"q_000431\",\n    \"response_text\": \"Summary: The bug in your code is the use of a mutable default argument (items=[]). In Python, default arguments are evaluated only once at the time of function definition, not every time the function is called.\\n\\nWhy it is problematic:\\n- When you call add_item('A'), it works as expected. \\n- However, if you call add_item('B') without providing a list, it will use the same list object that now contains 'A'. \\n- This causes the list to persist and grow across different function calls, which is usually unintended behavior.\\n\\nCorrected Version:\\n\\ndef add_item(name, items=None):\\n    if items is None:\\n        items = []\\n    items.append(name)\\n    return items\\n\\nExplanation of the fix:\\n- By setting the default to None (an immutable type), you ensure that a new list is created inside the function scope every time the function is called without an explicit list argument.\\n\\nSources:\\n- https://docs.python-guide.org/writing/gotchas/#mutable-default-arguments\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"query_id\": \"q_000432\",\n    \"response_text\": \"Summary: To ensure accurate outdoor run tracking, your Apple Watch relies on a combination of its own hardware and GPS data from your paired iPhone.\\n\\nRequired iPhone Settings:\\n1. Location Services: Go to Settings > Privacy & Security > Location Services. Ensure it is toggled On.\\n2. Apple Watch Workout: In the list of apps under Location Services, find Apple Watch Workout and set it to While Using the App.\\n3. System Services: Scroll to the bottom of the Location Services page and tap System Services. Ensure Motion Calibration & Distance is toggled On.\\n\\nCalibration Steps:\\n- Wear your Apple Watch and go to a flat, open outdoor area with good GPS reception.\\n- Open the Workout app on your watch.\\n- Start an Outdoor Walk or Outdoor Run.\\n- Walk or run at your normal pace for about 20 minutes.\\n- This process calibrates the accelerometer by learning your stride length at different speeds, improving accuracy when GPS is unavailable or weak.\\n\\nIcons:\\n- GPS: https://img.icons8.com/ios-filled/50/marker.png\\n- Fitness: https://img.icons8.com/ios-filled/5", "thoughtSignature": ""}], "role": "model"}, "finishReason": "MAX_TOKENS", "index": 0}], "usageMetadata": {"promptTokenCount": 2301, "candidatesTokenCount": 5082, "totalTokenCount": 10489, "promptTokensDetails": [{"modality": "TEXT", "tokenCount": 2301}], "thoughtsTokenCount": 3106}, "modelVersion": "gemini-3-flash-preview", "responseId": "u_qAabi5JJWUjuMPjIyHYA"}}
{"prompt_hash": "941496d441c2fcae098cdb69de600ac0fb55f747ca5f0f5a2da8c46519dc3534", "text": "[\n  {\n    \"query_id\": \"q_000841\",\n    \"response_text\": \"UI Layout Design: QR Scanner App\\n\\nAssumptions: The design is for a standard smartphone aspect ratio (19.5:9).\\n\\nScreen Structure:\\n- Top Bar: Transparent overlay containing a Flash Toggle icon (top right) and an Information icon (top left).\\n- Central Viewfinder: A large square frame in the center of the screen with glowing corner brackets. The area outside the frame is slightly dimmed.\\n- Scanning Animation: A horizontal laser line (green or blue) moving vertically within the viewfinder.\\n- Gallery Button: A circular button with an Image icon located directly below the viewfinder for uploading saved codes.\\n- Bottom Navigation Bar: A fixed white or dark grey bar with three distinct sections.\\n\\nIcons:\\n- Scan: camera_icon_active\\n- History: clock_history_icon\\n- Settings: gear_settings_icon\\n\\nQuick Actions:\\n[Button: Toggle Flash] action://camera/flash\\n[Button: Open Gallery] action://system/gallery\\n\\nSources:\\n- https://material.io/design\\n- https://developer.apple.com/design/human-interface-guidelines\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"query_id\": \"q_000842\",\n    \"response_text\": \"Wi-Fi Connection Details\\n\\nParsed Data:\\n- Network Name (SSID): CoffeeShop_Guest\\n- Security Type: WPA\\n- Password: LatteArt2024\\n- Hidden Network: No\\n\\nConnection Card:\\n- Title: Wi-Fi Network Found\\n- Icon: wifi_signal_icon\\n- SSID Label: CoffeeShop_Guest\\n- Security Label: WPA/WPA2 Personal\\n- Password Label: LatteArt2024 (Masked by default with eye icon to reveal)\\n\\nQuick Actions:\\n[Button: Connect Now] wifi://connect?s=CoffeeShop_Guest&t=WPA&p=LatteArt2024\\n[Button: Copy Password] action://copy/LatteArt2024\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"query_id\": \"q_000843\",\n    \"response_text\": \"Process: How Cameras Decode QR Codes\\n\\nStep 1: Image Acquisition\\nThe camera sensor captures a frame. The software converts it to grayscale and applies a threshold to create a high-contrast black-and-white image.\\n\\nStep 2: Finder Pattern Localization\\nThe software searches for the three large square 'finder patterns' (position detection patterns) in the corners. These are unique 1:1:3:1:1 ratio structures (black-white-black-white-black) that allow the scanner to identify the code's location, size, and orientation, even if the image is tilted or upside down.\\n\\nStep 3: Alignment and Timing\\nSmall alignment patterns and timing lines (alternating black/white modules between finders) help the software correct for perspective distortion and determine the grid size (Version).\\n\\nStep 4: Format and Version Reading\\nThe scanner reads the specific modules near the finder patterns to determine the error correction level and the mask pattern used.\\n\\nStep 5: Data Decoding\\nThe software removes the mask, reads the binary data modules in a zigzag pattern, and applies Reed-Solomon error correction to output the final string.\\n\\nSources:\\n- https://www.qrcode.com/en/about/howtoread.html\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"query_id\": \"q_000844\",\n    \"response_text\": \"QR Code Error Correction Comparison\\n\\nTable: Error Correction Levels\\nLevel | Data Restoration Capacity (Approx) | Recommended Use Case\\nL (Low) | 7% | Clean digital displays, high density data\\nM (Medium) | 15% | Standard consumer packaging, business cards\\nQ (Quartile) | 25% | Industrial environments, small labels\\nH (High) | 30% | Marketing posters, outdoor signage, high-wear items\\n\\nRecommendation for Marketing Poster:\\nLevel H (High) is the best choice. Marketing posters are often exposed to outdoor elements, physical scratching, or might be partially covered by stickers/graffiti. Level H ensures the code remains functional even if nearly a third of it is damaged.\\n\\nIcons:\\n- Level L: check_circle_outline\\n- Level H: shield_icon\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"query_id\": \"q_000845\",\n    \"response_text\": \"Security Best Practices: Hotel QR Check-in\\n\\nAssumptions: The hotel uses dynamic QR codes printed on physical signage or digital displays at the front desk.\\n\\nSecurity Checklist:\\n- Domain Verification: Ensure the QR code points to the hotel's official domain (e.g., hotelkyoto.com/checkin) rather than a generic URL shortener.\\n- Physical Inspection: Staff should regularly check physical QR stickers to ensure they haven't been covered by malicious 'quishing' stickers.\\n- Use SSL/TLS: The destination site must use HTTPS to encrypt guest data and provide a visual trust indicator (padlock icon) in the browser.\\n- Visual Branding: Incorporate the hotel logo into the QR code design (beautification) to make it harder for attackers to swap with a generic code.\\n- Clear Instructions: Display the expected destination URL in plain text next to the QR code so guests can verify before clicking.\\n\\nQuick Actions:\\n[Button: Security Audit Guide] https://www.owasp.org/index.php/Main_Page\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"query_id\": \"q_000846\",\n    \"response_text\": \"vCard 3.0 Content\\n\\nCopy and paste the following string into a QR generator:\\n\\nBEGIN:VCARD\\nVERSION:3.0\\nFN:Elena Rossi\\nN:Rossi;Elena;;;\\nTITLE:Senior Architect\\nORG:Milano Designs\\nEMAIL;TYPE=INTERNET;TYPE=WORK:elena.rossi@milanodesigns.it\\nTEL;TYPE=CELL:+39 02 123 4567\\nEND:VCARD\\n\\nQuick Actions:\\n[Button: Generate QR] https://www.qr-code-generator.com/\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"query_id\": \"q_000847\",\n    \"response_text\": \"UI Mockup: QR Scan History\\n\\nScreen Layout:\\n- Header: 'Scan History' with a 'Clear All' button.\\n- List Item Structure: Icon (Left), Text Content (Center), Date/Time (Right).\\n\\nExample History Items (Date: 2024-08-15):\\nOption 1: Wi-Fi Icon | CoffeeShop_Guest | 09:15 AM\\nOption 2: URL Icon | https://www.google.com/maps... | 10:30 AM\\nOption 3: Contact Icon | Elena Rossi - Senior Archite... | 12:45 PM\\nOption 4: URL Icon | https://www.restaurant-menu.c... | 07:20 PM\\nOption 5: Wi-Fi Icon | Hotel_Guest_Network | 11:55 PM\\n\\nIcons:\\n- URL: link_icon\\n- Wi-Fi: wifi_icon\\n- Contact: person_icon\\n\\nQuick Actions:\\n[Button: Export History] action://history/export\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"query_id\": \"q_000848\",\n    \"response_text\": \"Warehouse Inventory Analysis\\n\\nData Summary:\\n- Item: Ergonomic Chair (SKU-9928)\\n- Location: Section A4\\n- Current Quantity: 42\\n\\nLogic:\\nThe app checks the 'quantity' field. Since 42 is less than 50, a 'Restock' alert is triggered.\\n\\nUI Alert Display:\\n- Status Header: LOW STOCK ALERT (Bright Orange/Red background)\\n- Main Display: 'Ergonomic Chair' in bold text.\\n- Detail: 'Quantity remaining: 42 (Threshold: 50)'\\n- Location: 'Find in Section A4'\\n- Action: A large 'Request Restock' button at the bottom.\\n\\nQuick Actions:\\n[Button: Request Restock] app://inventory/restock?id=SKU-9928\\n[Button: View Section Map] app://warehouse/map?section=A4\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"query_id\": \"q_000849\",\n    \"response_text\": \"Restaurant Landing Page Layout\\n\\nAssumptions: The restaurant is a mid-scale dining establishment in NYC.\\n\\nUI Sections:\\n- Header: Restaurant Name & Logo with current hours.\\n- Section 1: Daily Specials (Card Carousel). Example: 'Truffle Pasta - $24'.\\n- Section 2: Main Course (Expandable List). Categories: Steaks, Seafood, Vegetarian.\\n- Section 3: Drinks (Grid View). Categories: Cocktails, Wine, Soft Drinks.\\n- Footer: Fixed Action Bar.\\n\\nQuick Actions:\\n[Button: Call Server] action://table/service_request\\n[Button: Pay Bill] action://table/checkout\\n[Button: View Allergens] https://restaurant.com/allergens\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"query_id\": \"q_000850\",\n    \"response_text\": \"Standard QR vs. Micro QR Comparison\\n\\nTable: Technical Differences\\nFeature | Standard QR Code | Micro QR Code\\nData Capacity | Up to 7,089 numeric chars | Up to 35 numeric chars\\nPhysical Size | Version 1 (21x21 modules) to 40 | M1 (11x11) to M4 (17x17)\\nFinder Patterns | 3 (top-left, top-right, bottom-left) | 1 (top-left corner only)\\nPrimary Use | General purpose, large data | Small electronics, medical parts\\n\\nSummary:\\nMicro QR codes are designed for applications where space is extremely limited. By using only one finder pattern and a smaller module grid, they significantly reduce the footprint but at the cost of much lower data storage capacity compared to the standard multi-finder pattern QR code.\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"query_id\": \"q_000851\",\n    \"response_text\": \"Guide: Scanning QR Codes on Android 13\\n\\nMethod 1: Using the Camera App\\n1. Open the built-in Camera app.\\n2. Point the lens at the QR code. Ensure the code is centered and well-lit.\\n3. A small link or text snippet will appear over the code or at the bottom of the viewfinder.\\n4. Tap the link to open the content.\\n\\nMethod 2: Using the Quick Settings Shortcut\\n1. Swipe down twice from the top of the screen to open the full Quick Settings panel.\\n2. Look for the 'QR Code Scanner' or 'Scan QR Code' tile. (If not visible, tap the Edit/Pencil icon to add it).\\n3. Tap the tile to open a dedicated, fast-loading scanner interface.\\n4. Scan the code and tap the resulting action button.\\n\\nIcons:\\n- Camera: camera_app_icon\\n- Quick Settings: quick_settings_tile_icon\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"query_id\": \"q_000852\",\n    \"response_text\": \"Museum Scan Result UI\\n\\nAssumptions: The exhibition features historical paintings with pre-recorded audio guides.\\n\\nUI Layout:\\n- Top: High-resolution thumbnail of the painting.\\n- Middle: Artist Biography (2 Paragraphs).\\n  Paragraph 1: Details about the artist's early life and movement.\\n  Paragraph 2: Context regarding the specific painting and its significance.\\n- Audio Section: Audio Player widget with Play/Pause, 10s Skip, and a Progress Bar.\\n- Bottom: 'Related Artworks' carousel.\\n\\nImages:\\n- Painting Thumbnail: https://museum-example.org.uk/images/painting_01_thumb.jpg\\n\\nQuick Actions:\\n[Button: Play Audio Guide] action://audio/play_tour_01\\n[Button: Save to Favorites] action://museum/save_art\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"query_id\": \"q_000853\",\n    \"response_text\": \"Technical Requirements: Dynamic QR Codes\\n\\nConcept:\\nA Dynamic QR code does not encode the final destination data. Instead, it encodes a 'Short URL' or 'Redirect URL' pointing to a central management server.\\n\\nProcess:\\n1. Generation: The user creates a code pointing to 'https://api.service.com/redirect/123'.\\n2. Printing: The code is printed on physical media.\\n3. Scanning: When a user scans the code, their device visits the redirect URL.\\n4. Server Role: The redirecting server looks up ID '123' in its database and identifies the current destination URL (e.g., 'https://website-v1.com').\\n5. Update: To change the destination, the owner simply updates the database entry for ID '123' to point to 'https://website-v2.com'. The physical QR code remains the same.\\n\\nTechnical Requirements:\\n- Persistent hosting for the redirecting server.\\n- Database to map short IDs to long URLs.\\n- Analytics engine (optional) to track scan counts and locations.\\n\\nSources:\\n- https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/QR_code#Dynamic_QR_codes\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"query_id\": \"q_000854\",\n    \"response_text\": \"MATMSG QR Action and UI\\n\\nAction:\\nThe smartphone should prompt the user to 'Compose Email' or 'Open in Mail App'.\\n\\nMockup of UI Prompt:\\n- Title: Send Email?\\n- To: support@techcorp.com\\n- Subject: Issue with Order #554\\n- Body Preview: Hello, I need help with my recent purchase...\\n- Buttons: [Cancel] [Compose Email]\\n\\nQuick Actions:\\n[Button: Send Email] mailto:support@techcorp.com?subject=Issue%20with%20Order%20%23554&body=Hello,%20I%20need%20help%20with%20my%20recent%20purchase.\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"query_id\": \"q_000855\",\n    \"response_text\": \"Troubleshooting: Low-Light QR Scanning\\n\\nHardware-Based Solutions:\\n1. Enable Flash: Use the camera's 