{"prompt_hash": "d9706a8845eeb4e036c6d8c46c847ee8fb792f59711b595a91e11e73640c8883", "text": "[\n  {\n    \"query_id\": \"q_000721\",\n    \"response_text\": \"Assumptions: This schedule assumes a focus on the Digital SAT format and utilizes 13 total hours of study per week.\\n\\n4-Week SAT Math Study Schedule\\n\\nWeek 1: Heart of Algebra\\n- Monday: Linear equations in one variable (solving and interpreting).\\n- Tuesday: Linear equations in two variables (graphing and slopes).\\n- Wednesday: Linear functions and modeling real-world scenarios.\\n- Thursday: Systems of linear equations (substitution and elimination).\\n- Friday: Linear inequalities in one or two variables.\\n- Saturday (3 Hours): Comprehensive review of Week 1 topics + 20-question practice set on Heart of Algebra.\\n\\nWeek 2: Problem Solving and Data Analysis\\n- Monday: Ratios, rates, and proportions.\\n- Tuesday: Percentages and units of measurement.\\n- Wednesday: One-variable data: distributions, mean, median, and mode.\\n- Thursday: Two-variable data: scatterplots and linear/exponential models.\\n- Friday: Probability and conditional probability.\\n- Saturday (3 Hours): Data analysis deep dive + full practice section focusing on tables/graphs.\\n\\nWeek 3: Passport to Advanced Math\\n- Monday: Operations with polynomials and factoring.\\n- Tuesday: Quadratic equations and functions (vertex and standard form).\\n- Wednesday: Exponential functions and radicals.\\n- Thursday: Rational expressions and equations.\\n- Friday: Function notation and transformations.\\n- Saturday (3 Hours): Advanced math problem set + review of complex quadratic word problems.\\n- Icons:\\n  - Algebra: https://img.icons8.com/ios/50/formula.png\\n  - Data: https://img.icons8.com/ios/50/combo-chart.png\\n\\nWeek 4: Geometry, Trigonometry, and Final Review\\n- Monday: Area and volume formulas.\\n- Tuesday: Lines, angles, and triangles (congruence and similarity).\\n- Wednesday: Right triangle trigonometry and the unit circle.\\n- Thursday: Circles (theorems and equations).\\n- Friday: Full review of weak areas identified in weeks 1-3.\\n- Saturday (3 Hours): Full-length SAT Math practice test under timed conditions + detailed error analysis.\\n\\nQuick Actions:\\n[Button: Practice on Khan Academy] https://www.khanacademy.org/sat\\n[Button: Official College Board Practice] https://satsuite.collegeboard.org/digital/digital-practice-questions/math\\n\\nSources:\\n- https://satsuite.collegeboard.org/sat/whats-on-the-test/math\\n- https://www.khanacademy.org/test-prep/sat-math\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"query_id\": \"q_000722\",\n    \"response_text\": \"Spanish Verb Conjugation: escribir (to write)\\n\\nConjugation Table\\n\\nPronoun | Present | Preterite | Imperfect | Future | Conditional\\nyo | escribo | escribí | escribía | escribiré | escribiría\\ntú | escribes | escribiste | escribías | escribirás | escribirías\\nél/ella/usted | escribe | escribió | escribía | escribirá | escribiría\\nnosotros | escribimos | escribimos | escribíamos | escribiremos | escribiríamos\\nvosotros | escribís | escribisteis | escribíais | escribiréis | escribiríais\\nellos/ellas/ustedes | escriben | escribieron | escribían | escribirán | escribirían\\n\\nQuick Actions:\\n[Button: Practice Spanish Verbs] https://www.spanishdict.com/conjugate/escribir\\n\\nSources:\\n- https://www.rae.es/\\n- https://www.spanishdict.com/\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"query_id\": \"q_000723\",\n    \"response_text\": \"Summary: Photosynthesis is the magical way plants make their own food! Instead of going to the grocery store, plants use sunlight, water, and air to create energy so they can grow big and strong.\\n\\nHow It Works:\\n1. Sunlight: The sun provides the energy. Think of it as the oven that cooks the food.\\n2. Chlorophyll: This is the green stuff inside leaves. It acts like a solar panel, catching the sunlight.\\n3. Water: Plants drink water through their roots. This is a key ingredient for their recipe.\\n4. Carbon Dioxide: This is a gas in the air that we breathe out. Plants breathe it in.\\n5. Glucose and Oxygen: After the plant 'cooks' the ingredients with sunlight, it makes Glucose (a type of sugar/food for the plant) and releases Oxygen into the air for us to breathe.\\n\\n5-Step Summary:\\n- Step 1: The plant drinks water through its roots.\\n- Step 2: The leaves take in carbon dioxide from the air.\\n- Step 3: Chlorophyll in the leaves catches energy from sunlight.\\n- Step 4: The sun's energy turns the water and carbon dioxide into sugar (glucose).\\n- Step 5: The plant keeps the sugar for food and lets out oxygen as a gift for us.\\n\\nImages:\\n- Photosynthesis Diagram: https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/db/Photosynthesis_en.svg/500px-Photosynthesis_en.svg.png\\n\\nSources:\\n- https://www.nasa.gov/audience/forstudents/k-4/dictionary/Photosynthesis.html\\n- https://kids.britannica.com/kids/article/photosynthesis/353617\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"query_id\": \"q_000724\",\n    \"response_text\": \"Solution for 3x^2 - 11x - 4 = 0 using the Quadratic Formula\\n\\nStep 1: Identify the coefficients\\na = 3\\nb = -11\\nc = -4\\n\\nStep 2: Calculate the discriminant (D)\\nThe formula is D = b^2 - 4ac.\\nD = (-11)^2 - 4(3)(-4)\\nD = 121 - (-48)\\nD = 121 + 48\\nD = 169\\n\\nStep 3: Apply the Quadratic Formula\\nx = [-b +/- sqrt(D)] / 2a\\nx = [-(-11) +/- sqrt(169)] / 2(3)\\nx = [11 +/- 13] / 6\\n\\nStep 4: Solve for both values of x\\nCalculation for x1:\\nx1 = (11 + 13) / 6\\nx1 = 24 / 6\\nx1 = 4\\n\\nCalculation for x2:\\nx2 = (11 - 13) / 6\\nx2 = -2 / 6\\nx2 = -1/3 (or approximately -0.33)\\n\\nFinal Values:\\nx = 4, x = -1/3\\n\\nSources:\\n- https://www.khanacademy.org/math/algebra/x2f8bb11595b61c86:quadratic-functions-equations/x2f8bb11595b61c86:quadratic-formula/a/quadratic-formula-review\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"query_id\": \"q_000725\",\n    \"response_text\": \"Lesson Plan: The Causes of World War I\\n\\nDuration: 60 Minutes\\nGrade Level: High School History\\n\\nI. Warm-up (5 Minutes)\\n- Activity: 'The Domino Effect' Discussion.\\n- Prompt: Ask students to think of a time a small argument between two people turned into a huge group conflict. Briefly discuss how alliances work in real life.\\n\\nII. Lecture Outline: The M.A.I.N. Causes (20 Minutes)\\n- Militarism: The glorification of military power and the arms race in Europe (e.g., naval rivalry between Britain and Germany).\\n- Alliances: The web of secret treaties (Triple Entente vs. Triple Alliance) that meant one small spark would involve everyone.\\n- Imperialism: Competition for colonies and resources in Africa and Asia leading to diplomatic tension.\\n- Nationalism: Intense patriotism and the desire for self-determination (focus on the Balkans and 'The Powder Keg of Europe').\\n- The Spark: Brief mention of the assassination of Archduke Franz Ferdinand.\\n\\nIII. Primary Source Analysis (25 Minutes)\\n- Activity: Analyzing the 'Blank Check' and Alliance Documents.\\n- Task: Students work in pairs to read excerpts from the Austro-Hungarian ultimatum to Serbia and the German 'Blank Check' telegram. \\n- Questions: How did these documents escalate the situation? Who was being aggressive and who was being defensive?\\n\\nIV. Wrap-up Discussion (10 Minutes)\\n- Question: Which of the M.A.I.N. factors do you believe was the most significant in making the war inevitable? Why?\\n- Exit Ticket: Write down one way the world might look today if the Triple Alliance had won.\\n\\nQuick Actions:\\n- [Button: Download Primary Sources] https://www.archives.gov/education/lessons/world-war-i\\n\\nSources:\\n- https://www.history.com/topics/world-war-i/world-war-i-history\\n- https://learning.blogs.nytimes.com/2014/06/27/the-causes-of-world-war-i/\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"query_id\": \"q_000726\",\n    \"response_text\": \"Comparison of Montessori and Waldorf Educational Philosophies\\n\\nEducational Philosophy Comparison Table\\n\\nCriterion | Montessori | Waldorf\\nClassroom Environment | Prepared, orderly, minimalist, and child-sized. | Home-like, warm, aesthetic, and features natural materials.\\nRole of the Teacher | An observer and guide who facilitates independent work. | A leader and storyteller who stays with the same group for years.\\nUse of Technology | Generally discouraged in early years; focus on tactile tools. | Strictly discouraged in early childhood; focus on imagination.\\nCurriculum Focus | Academics (math/lang) through self-directed 'work' cycles. | Holistic (arts/spirit/physical) through creative 'play'.\\nAge Grouping | Multi-age classrooms (e.g., 3-6 years old together). | Single-age classrooms (peers of the same age).\\n\\nKey Differences Summary:\\nMontessori emphasizes independence, scientific observation, and individual academic mastery. Waldorf emphasizes the collective, imagination, and the developmental stages of the child's spirit and creativity.\\n\\nSources:\\n- https://www.amshq.org/\\n- https://www.waldorfeducation.org/\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"query_id\": \"q_000727\",\n    \"response_text\": \"GRE Vocabulary: Criticism and Praise\\n\\nWords of Criticism:\\n1. Castigate (verb): To reprimand severely.\\n   Example: The director castigated the lead actor for arriving late to every rehearsal.\\n2. Invective (noun): Insulting, abusive, or highly critical language.\\n   Example: The politician's speech was filled with invective against his opponents.\\n3. Vituperation (noun): Bitter and abusive language.\\n   Example: No amount of vituperation from the critics could dampen the artist's spirits.\\n4. Opprobrium (noun): Harsh criticism or censure.\\n   Example: The company faced public opprobrium after the environmental scandal was revealed.\\n5. Diatribe (noun): A forceful and bitter verbal attack against someone or something.\\n   Example: The journalist launched a lengthy diatribe against the new tax policy.\\n6. Berate (verb): To scold or criticize someone angrily.\\n   Example: The coach began to berate the players for their lack of effort in the second half.\\n7. Lambaste (verb): To criticize someone or something harshly.\\n   Example: The professor lambasted the student's thesis for lacking original research.\\n\\nWords of Praise:\\n8. Adulation (noun): Excessive admiration or praise.\\n   Example: The pop star basked in the adulation of her screaming fans.\\n9. Encomium (noun): A speech or piece of writing that praises someone or something highly.\\n   Example: The retired general received a glowing encomium during the ceremony.\\n10. Laudatory (adj): Expressing praise and commendation.\\n    Example: The reviewer wrote a laudatory article about the chef's innovative menu.\\n11. Venerate (verb): To regard with great respect; revere.\\n    Example: Many cultures venerate their ancestors through annual festivals.\\n12. Panegyric (noun): A public speech or published text in praise of someone or something.\\n    Example: The poet delivered a moving panegyric at the funeral of his mentor.\\n13. Accolade (noun): An award or privilege granted as a special honor or as an acknowledgment of merit.\\n    Example: Winning the Nobel Prize is the ultimate accolade for any scientist.\\n14. Eulogize (verb): To praise highly in speech or writing, especially in a funeral oration.\\n    Example: Friends gathered to eulogize the local hero for his years of service.\\n15. Approbation (noun): Approval or praise.\\n    Example: The new opera was met with great approbation from the audience.\\n\\nQuick Actions:\\n[Button: Practice GRE Vocab] https://www.maguosh.com/gre-vocabulary-flashcards\\n\\nSources:\\n- https://www.merriam-webster.com/\\n- https://www.ets.org/gre\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"query_id\": \"q_000728\",\n    \"response_text\": \"12-Week Syllabus: Introduction to Python Programming\\n\\nWeek | Theme | Key Concepts | Suggested Mini-Project\\n1 | Getting Started | Syntax, Variables, Data Types | Hello World & Personal Bio\\n2 | Flow Control | If/Else, Logical Operators, Indentation | Grade Calculator