Calculate the discriminant (D)\\nThe formula is D = b^2 - 4ac.\\nD = (-11)^2 - 4(3)(-4)\\nD = 121 - (-48)\\nD = 121 + 48\\nD = 169\\n\\nStep 3: Apply the Quadratic Formula\\nx = [-b +/- sqrt(D)] / 2a\\nx = [-(-11) +/- sqrt(169)] / 2(3)\\nx = [11 +/- 13] / 6\\n\\nStep 4: Solve for both values of x\\nCalculation for x1:\\nx1 = (11 + 13) / 6\\nx1 = 24 / 6\\nx1 = 4\\n\\nCalculation for x2:\\nx2 = (11 - 13) / 6\\nx2 = -2 / 6\\nx2 = -1/3 (or approximately -0.33)\\n\\nFinal Values:\\nx = 4, x = -1/3\\n\\nSources:\\n- https://www.khanacademy.org/math/algebra/x2f8bb11595b61c86:quadratic-functions-equations/x2f8bb11595b61c86:quadratic-formula/a/quadratic-formula-review\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"query_id\": \"q_000725\",\n    \"response_text\": \"Lesson Plan: The Causes of World War I\\n\\nDuration: 60 Minutes\\nGrade Level: High School History\\n\\nI. Warm-up (5 Minutes)\\n- Activity: 'The Domino Effect' Discussion.\\n- Prompt: Ask students to think of a time a small argument between two people turned into a huge group conflict. Briefly discuss how alliances work in real life.\\n\\nII. Lecture Outline: The M.A.I.N. Causes (20 Minutes)\\n- Militarism: The glorification of military power and the arms race in Europe (e.g., naval rivalry between Britain and Germany).\\n- Alliances: The web of secret treaties (Triple Entente vs. Triple Alliance) that meant one small spark would involve everyone.\\n- Imperialism: Competition for colonies and resources in Africa and Asia leading to diplomatic tension.\\n- Nationalism: Intense patriotism and the desire for self-determination (focus on the Balkans and 'The Powder Keg of Europe').\\n- The Spark: Brief mention of the assassination of Archduke Franz Ferdinand.\\n\\nIII. Primary Source Analysis (25 Minutes)\\n- Activity: Analyzing the 'Blank Check' and Alliance Documents.\\n- Task: Students work in pairs to read excerpts from the Austro-Hungarian ultimatum to Serbia and the German 'Blank Check' telegram. \\n- Questions: How did these documents 