d severely.\\n   Example: The director castigated the lead actor for arriving late to every rehearsal.\\n2. Invective (noun): Insulting, abusive, or highly critical language.\\n   Example: The politician's speech was filled with invective against his opponents.\\n3. Vituperation (noun): Bitter and abusive language.\\n   Example: No amount of vituperation from the critics could dampen the artist's spirits.\\n4. Opprobrium (noun): Harsh criticism or censure.\\n   Example: The company faced public opprobrium after the environmental scandal was revealed.\\n5. Diatribe (noun): A forceful and bitter verbal attack against someone or something.\\n   Example: The journalist launched a lengthy diatribe against the new tax policy.\\n6. Berate (verb): To scold or criticize someone angrily.\\n   Example: The coach began to berate the players for their lack of effort in the second half.\\n7. Lambaste (verb): To criticize someone or something harshly.\\n   Example: The professor lambasted the student's thesis for lacking original research.\\n\\nWords of Praise:\\n8. Adulation (noun): Excessive admiration or praise.\\n   Example: The pop star basked in the adulation of her screaming fans.\\n9. Encomium (noun): A speech or piece of writing that praises someone or something highly.\\n   Example: The retired general received a glowing encomium during the ceremony.\\n10. Laudatory (adj): Expressing praise and commendation.\\n    Example: The reviewer wrote a laudatory article about the chef's innovative menu.\\n11. Venerate (verb): To regard with great respect; revere.\\n    Example: Many cultures venerate their ancestors through annual festivals.\\n12. Panegyric (noun): A public speech or published text in praise of someone or something.\\n    Example: The poet delivered a moving panegyric at the funeral of his mentor.\\n13. Accolade (noun): An award or privilege granted as a special honor or as an acknowledgment of merit.\\n    Example: Winning the Nobel Prize is the ultimate accolade for any scientist.\\n14. Eulogize (verb): To praise highly in speech or writing, especially in a funeral oration.\\n    Example: Friends gathered to eulogize the local hero for his years of service.\\n15. Approbation (noun): Approval or praise.\\n    Example: The new opera was met with great approbation from the audience.\\n\\nQuick Actions:\\n[Button: Practice GRE Vocab] https://www.maguosh.com/gre-vocabulary-flashcards\\n\\nSources:\\n- https://www.merriam-webster.com/\\n- https://www.ets.org/gre\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"query_id\": \"q_000728\",\n    \"response_text\": \"12-Week Syllabus: Introduction to Python Programming\\n\\nWeek | Theme | Key Concepts | Suggested Mini-Project\\n1 | Getting Started | Syntax, Variables, Data Types | Hello World & Personal Bio\\n2 | Flow Control | If/Else, Logical Operators, Indentation | Grade Calculator\\n3 | Loops | For loops, While loops, Range function | Guess the Number Game\\n4 | Functions | Definition, Parameters, Return values | Simple Unit Converter\\n5 | Data Structures I | Lists, Indexing, Slicing | Grocery List Manager\\n6 | Data Structures II | Dictionaries, Tuples, Sets | Contact Phonebook\\n7 | File I/O | Reading files, Writing files, Context managers | Diary Entry Logger\\n8 | Error Handling | Try/Except blocks, Debugging, Exceptions | Robust Calculator\\n9 | Modules & Libraries | Import, Math, Random, Datetime | Random Password Generator\\n10 | Intro to OOP | Classes, Objects, Attributes | Virtual Pet Simulator\\n11 | Intermediate OOP | Methods, Inheritance, Encapsulation | Library Catalog System\\n12 | Final Integration | Project Planning, Clean Code, Review | Personal Expense Tracker\\n\\nQuick Actions:\\n[Button: Download Python] https://www.python.org/downloads/\\n[Button: Python Documentation] https://docs.python.org/3/\\n\\nSources:\\n- https://www.python.org/\\n- https://www.w3schools.com/python/\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"query_id\": \"q_000729\",\n    \"response_text\": \"Newton's Three Laws of Motion\\n\\n1. The Law of Inertia\\n- Formal Definition: An object at rest stays at rest and an object in motion stays in motion with the same speed and in the same direction unless acted upon by an unbalanced force.\\n- Plain English: Things keep doing what they're already doing unless something pushes or pulls them.\\n- Example: A soccer ball sitting on the grass won't move until a player kicks it.\\n\\n2. The Law of Acceleration (F=ma)\\n- Formal Definition: The acceleration of an object is directly proportional to the net force acting on it and inversely proportional to its mass.\\n- Plain English: If you push something harder, it goes faster. If the object is heavier, you need to push it even harder to get it to move.\\n- Example: It is much easier to accelerate a small toy car by pushing it than it is to push a real, full-sized car.\\n\\n3. The Law of Action and Reaction\\n- Formal Definition: For every action, there is an equal and opposite reaction.\\n- Plain English: Whenever you push something, it pushes back on you just as hard in the opposite direction.\\n- Example: When you kick a soccer ball, the ball flies forward, but your foot feels the force of the ball pushing back against it.\\n\\nIcons:\\n- Physics: https://img.icons8.com/ios/50/physics.png\\n- Motion: https://img.icons8.com/ios/50/fast-forward.png\\n\\nSources:\\n- https://www.nasa.gov/stem-ed-resources/newtons-laws-of-motion.html\\n- https://www.britannica.com/science/Newtons-laws-of-motion\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"query_id\": \"q_000730\",\n    \"response_text\": \"Plot Summary of 'Animal Farm'\\n\\nParagraph 1: The story begins on Manor Farm, where the animals, led by Old Major the pig, revolt against their neglectful human farmer, Mr. Jones. They establish 'Animalism,' a philosophy of equality, and rename the farm 'Animal Farm.' Initially, the animals thrive, working together and following the Seven Commandments, with the most important being 'All animals are equal.'\\n\\nParagraph 2: Power struggles soon emerge