e\\nData Capacity | Up to 7,089 numeric chars | Up to 35 numeric chars\\nPhysical Size | Version 1 (21x21 modules) to 40 | M1 (11x11) to M4 (17x17)\\nFinder Patterns | 3 (top-left, top-right, bottom-left) | 1 (top-left corner only)\\nPrimary Use | General purpose, large data | Small electronics, medical parts\\n\\nSummary:\\nMicro QR codes are designed for applications where space is extremely limited. By using only one finder pattern and a smaller module grid, they significantly reduce the footprint but at the cost of much lower data storage capacity compared to the standard multi-finder pattern QR code.\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"query_id\": \"q_000851\",\n    \"response_text\": \"Guide: Scanning QR Codes on Android 13\\n\\nMethod 1: Using the Camera App\\n1. Open the built-in Camera app.\\n2. Point the lens at the QR code. Ensure the code is centered and well-lit.\\n3. A small link or text snippet will appear over the code or at the bottom of the viewfinder.\\n4. Tap the link to open the content.\\n\\nMethod 2: Using the Quick Settings Shortcut\\n1. Swipe down twice from the top of the screen to open the full Quick Settings panel.\\n2. Look for the 'QR Code Scanner' or 'Scan QR Code' tile. (If not visible, tap the Edit/Pencil icon to add it).\\n3. Tap the tile to open a dedicated, fast-loading scanner interface.\\n4. Scan the code and tap the resulting action button.\\n\\nIcons:\\n- Camera: camera_app_icon\\n- Quick Settings: quick_settings_tile_icon\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"query_id\": \"q_000852\",\n    \"response_text\": \"Museum Scan Result UI\\n\\nAssumptions: The exhibition features historical paintings with pre-recorded audio guides.\\n\\nUI Layout:\\n- Top: High-resolution thumbnail of the painting.\\n- Middle: Artist Biography (2 Paragraphs).\\n  Paragraph 1: Details about the artist's early life and movement.\\n  Paragraph 2: Context regarding the specific painting and its significance.\\n- Audio Section: Audio Player widget with Play/Pause, 10s Skip, and a Progress Bar.\\n- Bottom: 'Related Artworks' carousel.\\n\\nImages:\\n- Painting Thumbnail: https://museum-example.org.uk/images/painting_01_thumb.jpg\\n\\nQuick Actions:\\n[Button: Play Audio Guide] action://audio/play_tour_01\\n[Button: Save to Favorites] action://museum/save_art\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"query_id\": \"q_000853\",\n    \"response_text\": \"Technical Requirements: Dynamic QR Codes\\n\\nConcept:\\nA Dynamic QR code does not encode the final destination data. Instead, it encodes a 'Short URL' or 'Redirect URL' pointing to a central management server.\\n\\nProcess:\\n1. Generation: The user creates a code pointing to 'https://api.service.com/redirect/123'.\\n2. Printing: The code is printed on physical media.\\n3. Scanning: When a user scans the code, their device visits the redirect URL.\\n4. Server Role: The redirecting server looks up ID '123' in its database and identifies the current destination URL (e.g., 'https://website-v1.com').\\n5. Update: To change the destination, the owner simply updates the database entry for ID '123' to point to 'https://website-v2.com'. The physical QR code remains the same.\\n\\nTechnical Requirements:\\n- Persistent hosting for the redirecting server.\\n- Database to map short IDs to long URLs.\\n- Analytics engine (optional) to track scan counts and locations.\\n\\nSources:\\n- https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/QR_code#Dynamic_QR_codes\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"query_id\": \"q_000854\",\n    \"response_text\": \"MATMSG QR Action and UI\\n\\nAction:\\nThe smartphone should prompt the user to 'Compose Email' or 'Open in Mail App'.\\n\\nMockup of UI Prompt:\\n- Title: Send Email?\\n- To: support@techcorp.com\\n- Subject: Issue with Order #554\\n- Body Preview: Hello, I need help with my recent purchase...\\n- Buttons: [Cancel] [Compose Email]\\n\\nQuick Actions:\\n[Button: Send Email] mailto:support@techcorp.com?subject=Issue%20with%20Order%20%23554&body=Hello,%20I%20need%20help%20with%20my%20recent%20purchase.\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"query_id\": \"q_000855\",\n    \"response_text\": \"Troubleshooting: Low-Light QR Scanning\\n\\nHardware-Based Solutions:\\n1. Enable Flash: Use the camera's LED flash to illuminate the code directly.\\n2. Adjust Distance: Move the phone closer or further away to allow the sensor's auto-focus to lock on the module edges.\\n3. External Lighting: Reposition the object or the user to avoid shadows cast by the device itself.\\n\\nSoftware-Based Techniques:\\n1. Histogram Equalization: Increase the contrast of the captured frame to make black modules stand out against grey/dark backgrounds.\\n2. Binarization Thresholding: Use adaptive thresholding to distinguish modules in unevenly lit environments (e.g., one side of the code is darker than the other).\\n\\nIcons:\\n- Flash: flash_on_icon\\n- Contrast: contrast_icon\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"query_id\": \"q_000856\",\n    \"response_text\": \"Real Estate QR Data Fields\\n\\nTo ensure a seamless experience for map boundaries and 3D tours, the QR code should ideally encode a URL containing these parameters or pointing to a landing page with this metadata:\\n\\nData Fields:\\n- Property ID: Unique identifier for the listing.\\n- Coordinates: Latitude and Longitude for precise map pinning.\\n- Boundary GeoJSON: Link to a file or data string defining property lines.\\n- Virtual Tour URL: Direct link to the Matterport or 3D hosting site.\\n- Agent Contact: vCard data for the listing agent.\\n\\nRecommended Encoding Format (URL):\\nhttps://agency.com.au/prop?id=SYD123&lat=-33.86&long=151.20&tour=true\\n\\nQuick Actions:\\n[Button: View Map] https://maps.google.com/?q=-33.86,151.20\\n[Button: Start 3D Tour] https://agency.com.au/tour/SYD123\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"query_id\": \"q_000857\",\n    \"response_text\": \"Comparison: QR Codes vs. NFC for Cafe Payments\\n\\nTable: Payment Method Comparison\\nFactor | QR Codes | NFC Tags\\nImplementation Cost | Very Low (Paper print) | Moderate (Hardware tags)\\nCustomer Ease | High (Camera scan) | Very High (Tap and go)\\nDurability | Low (Can smudge/tear) | High (Encased in plastic/metal)\\nHardware Required | Smartphone Camera | NFC-enabled Smartphone\\n\\nSummary:\\nQR codes are better for cafes with frequent menu/payment link changes and low budgets. NFC tags are superior for high-traffic environments where speed and durability are critical, though they require more initial investment per table or terminal.\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"query_id\": \"q_000858\",\n    \"response_text\": \"UI Layout: QR Code Generator\\n\\nSections:\\n- Input Field: 'Enter URL or Text' (Multi-line support).\\n- Color Customization:\\n  - Foreground Color: [Color Picker Widget]\\n  - Background Color: [Color Picker Widget]\\n- Layout Settings:\\n  - Quiet Zone Size: [Slider: 1px to 20px]\\n  - Error Correction: [Dropdown: L, M, Q, H]\\n- Preview Area: Real-time updating QR code image.\\n- Export Section: [Button: Download PNG] [Button: Download SVG]\\n\\nQuick Actions:\\n[Button: Generate] action://generator/run\\n[Button: Download PNG] action://generator/export/png\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"query_id\": \"q_000859\",\n    \"response_text\": \"Logic Flow: Ticket Validation (Berlin Transport)\\n\\nInput String: TICKET_ID:88392;EXP:2024-06-30T23:59:59Z;ZONE:AB\\n\\nValidation Steps:\\n1. Parse String: Extract TICKET_ID, EXP (Expiry), and ZONE.\\n2. Check Expiry: Compare current system time (UTC) with EXP.\\n   - If Current Time > 2024-06-30T23:59:59Z -> Result: EXPIRED.\\n3. Check Zone: Compare ZONE:AB with the scanner's current location.\\n   - If Scanner Location is outside Zone A or B -> Result: INVALID ZONE.\\n4. Check Authenticity: Verify TICKET_ID against the central database (if online) or check digital signature.\\n5. Final Output:\\n   - If all checks pass: Display GREEN screen with 'VALID'.\\n   - If any check fails: Display RED screen with specific error (e.g., 'TICKET EXPIRED').\\n\\nAssumptions: The scanner has access to accurate UTC time.\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"query_id\": \"q_000860\",\n 