d fellow mariners, we gather today not to mourn the setting of the sun, but to celebrate the light that guided us home for forty years. Captain Barnaby was more than the keeper of the Quoddy Reach Light; he was the heartbeat of this coast.\\n\\nBarnaby didn't talk much—he let the foghorn and the rhythmic pulse of the beacon do the talking for him. He lived a life measured in tides and wick-trimmings. But if you wanted to see him smile, all you had to do was mention two things: his dog, Barnacles, and a fresh blueberry pie. \\n\\nBarnacles was his constant shadow, a scruffy terrier who probably knew the shipping lanes better than most naval officers. They were a pair—one bearded and salt-stained, the other furry and perpetually smelling of kelp. And that pie... Barnaby claimed the secret to a long life was a slice of Maine blueberry pie every evening, purple stains on his beard be damned.\\n\\nHe stood watch through the Great Nor'easter of '98 and a thousand nameless gales, never letting that light flicker. He’s gone over the horizon now, but as long as the waves hit these rocks, we’ll remember the Captain. Barnacles is in good hands, and there’s a pie waiting on the windowsill of heaven for him. Fair winds and following seas, Captain.\\n\\nICONS\\n- Lighthouse: https://icons.example/lighthouse\\n- Anchor: https://icons.example/anchor\\n- Dog: https://icons.example/dog-paw\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"query_id\": \"q_000460\",\n    \"response_text\": \"LINKEDIN POST: A NEW CHAPTER\\n\\nAfter 15 years of chasing tickers, 80-hour weeks, and the relentless hum of New York City, I’ve decided to trade the skyscrapers for the Big Sky. \\n\\nI am officially announcing my departure from the finance world to pursue a full-time career as a landscape photographer in Montana. \\n\\nFor a long time, I measured my success by the size of my portfolio and the speed of my promotion. But the cost was high—my mental health, my creativity, and my time. We often talk about 'investing' in our futu