 use of space on tiny components like surgical tools or microchips.\\n\\nSources:\\n- https://www.gs1.org/standards/barcodes/datamatrix\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"query_id\": \"q_000866\",\n", "raw": {"candidates": [{"content": {"parts": [{"text": "[\n  {\n    \"query_id\": \"q_000841\",\n    \"response_text\": \"UI Layout Design: QR Scanner App\\n\\nAssumptions: The design is for a standard smartphone aspect ratio (19.5:9).\\n\\nScreen Structure:\\n- Top Bar: Transparent overlay containing a Flash Toggle icon (top right) and an Information icon (top left).\\n- Central Viewfinder: A large square frame in the center of the screen with glowing corner brackets. The area outside the frame is slightly dimmed.\\n- Scanning Animation: A horizontal laser line (green or blue) moving vertically within the viewfinder.\\n- Gallery Button: A circular button with an Image icon located directly below the viewfinder for uploading saved codes.\\n- Bottom Navigation Bar: A fixed white or dark grey bar with three distinct sections.\\n\\nIcons:\\n- Scan: camera_icon_active\\n- History: clock_history_icon\\n- Settings: gear_settings_icon\\n\\nQuick Actions:\\n[Button: Toggle Flash] action://camera/flash\\n[Button: Open Gallery] action://system/gallery\\n\\nSources:\\n- https://material.io/design\\n- https://developer.apple.com/design/human-interface-guidelines\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"query_id\": \"q_000842\",\n    \"response_text\": \"Wi-Fi Connection Details\\n\\nParsed Data:\\n- Network Name (SSID): CoffeeShop_Guest\\n- Security Type: WPA\\n- Password: LatteArt2024\\n- Hidden Network: No\\n\\nConnection Card:\\n- Title: Wi-Fi Network Found\\n- Icon: wifi_signal_icon\\n- SSID Label: CoffeeShop_Guest\\n- Security Label: WPA/WPA2 Personal\\n- Password Label: LatteArt2024 (Masked by default with eye icon to reveal)\\n\\nQuick Actions:\\n[Button: Connect Now] wifi://connect?s=CoffeeShop_Guest&t=WPA&p=LatteArt2024\\n[Button: Copy Password] action://copy/LatteArt2024\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"query_id\": \"q_000843\",\n    \"response_text\": \"Process: How Cameras Decode QR Codes\\n\\nStep 1: Image Acquisition\\nThe camera sensor captures a frame. The software converts it to grayscale and applies a threshold to create a high-contrast black-and-white image.\\n\\nStep 2: Finder Pattern Localization\\nThe software searches for the three large square 'finder patterns' (position detection patterns) in the corners. These are unique 1:1:3:1:1 ratio structures (black-white-black-white-black) that allow the scanner to identify the code's location, size, and orientation, even if the image is tilted or upside down.\\n\\nStep 3: Alignment and Timing\\nSmall alignment patterns and timing lines (alternating black/white modules between finders) help the software correct for perspective distortion and determine the grid size (Version).\\n\\nStep 4: Format and Version Reading\\nThe scanner reads the specific modules near the finder patterns to determine the error correction level and the mask pattern used.\\n\\nStep 5: Data Decoding\\nThe software removes the mask, reads the binary data modules in a zigzag pattern, and applies Reed-Solomon error correction to output the final string.\\n\\nSources:\\n- https://www.qrcode.com/en/about/howtoread.html\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"query_id\": \"q_000844\",\n    \"response_text\": \"QR Code Error Correction Comparison\\n\\nTable: Error Correction Levels\\nLevel | Data Restoration Capacity (Approx) | Recommended Use Case\\nL (Low) | 7% | Clean digital displays, high density data\\nM (Medium) | 15% | Standard consumer packaging, business cards\\nQ (Quartile) | 25% | Industrial environments, small labels\\nH (High) | 30% | Marketing posters, outdoor signage, high-wear items\\n\\nRecommendation for Marketing Poster:\\nLevel H (High) is the best choice. Marketing posters are often exposed to outdoor elements, physical scratching, or might be partially covered by stickers/graffiti. Level H ensures the code remains functional even if nearly a third of it is damaged.\\n\\nIcons:\\n- Level L: check_circle_outline\\n- Level H: shield_icon\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"query_id\": \"q_000845\",\n    \"response_text\": \"Security Best Practices: Hotel QR Check-in\\n\\nAssumptions: The hotel uses dynamic QR codes printed on physical signage or digital displays at the front desk.\\n\\nSecurity Checklist:\\n- Domain Verification: Ensure the QR code points to the hotel's official domain (e.g., hotelkyoto.com/checkin) rather than a generic URL shortener.\\n- Physical Inspection: Staff should regularly check physical QR stickers to ensure they haven't been covered by malicious 'quishing' stickers.\\n- Use SSL/TLS: The destination site must use HTTPS to encrypt guest data and provide a visual trust indicator (padlock icon) in the browser.\\n- Visual Branding: Incorporate the hotel logo into the QR code design (beautification) to make it harder for attackers to swap with a generic code.\\n- Clear Instructions: Display the expected destination URL in plain text next to the QR code so guests can verify before clicking.\\n\\nQuick Actions:\\n[Button: Security Audit Guide] https://www.owasp.org/index.php/Main_Page\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"query_id\": \"q_000846\",\n    \"response_text\": \"vCard 3.0 Content\\n\\nCopy and paste the following string into a QR generator:\\n\\nBEGIN:VCARD\\nVERSION:3.0\\nFN:Elena Rossi\\nN:Rossi;Elena;;;\\nTITLE:Senior Architect\\nORG:Milano Designs\\nEMAIL;TYPE=INTERNET;TYPE=WORK:elena.rossi@milanodesigns.it\\nTEL;TYPE=CELL:+39 02 123 4567\\nEND:VCARD\\n\\nQuick Actions:\\n[Button: Generate QR] https://www.qr-code-generator.com/\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"query_id\": \"q_000847\",\n    \"response_text\": \"UI Mockup: QR Scan History\\n\\nScreen Layout:\\n- Header: 'Scan History' with a 'Clear All' button.\\n- List Item Structure: Icon (Left), Text Content (Center), Date/Time (Right).\\n\\nExample History Items (Date: 2024-08-15):\\nOption 1: Wi-Fi Icon | CoffeeShop_Guest | 09:15 AM\\nOption 2: URL Icon | https://www.google.co