{"prompt_hash": "96c50eda32abe0486af5c22858707ce4d17e3a1febb71235302263218f08f720", "text": "[\n  {\n    \"version\": \"v0.9\",\n    \"createSurface\": {\n      \"surfaceId\": \"music-theory-circle-of-fifths\",\n      \"catalogId\": \"https://genui.local/specification/v0_9/standard_catalog.json\",\n      \"sendDataModel\": false\n    }\n  },\n  {\n    \"version\": \"v0.9\",\n    \"updateComponents\": {\n      \"surfaceId\": \"music-theory-circle-of-fifths\",\n      \"components\": [\n        {\n          \"id\": \"root\",\n          \"component\": \"Column\",\n          \"children\": [\n            \"header-row\",\n            \"concept-card\",\n            \"key-sig-card\",\n            \"guitar-section-col\"\n          ],\n          \"spacing\": 16\n        },\n        {\n          \"id\": \"header-row\",\n          \"component\": \"Row\",\n          \"align\": \"center\",\n          \"children\": [\n            \"theory-icon\",\n            \"main-title\"\n          ]\n        },\n        {\n          \"id\": \"theory-icon\",\n          \"component\": \"Image\",\n          \"url\": \"/music-notation.png\",\n          \"variant\": \"icon\"\n        },\n        {\n          \"id\": \"main-title\",\n          \"component\": \"Text\",\n          \"text\": \"The Circle of Fifths: Music Theory Guide\",\n          \"variant\": \"h1\"\n        },\n        {\n          \"id\": \"concept-card\",\n          \"component\": \"Card\",\n          \"child\": \"concept-col\"\n        },\n        {\n          \"id\": \"concept-col\",\n          \"component\": \"Column\",\n          \"children\": [\n            \"concept-title\",\n            \"concept-body\"\n          ]\n        },\n        {\n          \"id\": \"concept-title\",\n          \"component\": \"Text\",\n          \"text\": \"Concept\",\n          \"variant\": \"h2\"\n        },\n        {\n          \"id\": \"concept-body\",\n          \"component\": \"Text\",\n          \"text\": \"The Circle of Fifths is a visual representation of the 12 tones of the chromatic scale, their corresponding key signatures, and the relationships between them. Moving clockwise, each note is a perfect fifth above the previous one (e.g., C to G).\",\n          \"variant\": \"body\"\n        },\n        {\n          \"id\": \"key-sig-card\",\n          \"component\": \"Card\",\n          \"child\": \"key-sig-col\"\n        },\n        {\n          \"id\": \"key-sig-col\",\n          \"component\": \"Column\",\n          \"children\": [\n            \"key-sig-title\",\n            \"key-sig-body\"\n          ]\n        },\n        {\n          \"id\": \"key-sig-title\",\n          \"component\": \"Text\",\n          \"text\": \"Determining Key Signatures\",\n          \"variant\": \"h2\"\n        },\n        {\n          \"id\": \"key-sig-body\",\n          \"component\": \"Text\",\n          \"text\": \"- **Top:** C Major (no sharps or flats).\\n- **Clockwise (Sharps):** Each step adds a sharp. G has 1 (#F), D has 2 (#F, #C), A has 3.\\n- **Counter-Clockwise (Flats):** Each step adds a flat. F has 1 (Bb), Bb has 2 (Bb, Eb).\",\n          \"variant\": \"body\"\n        },\n        {\n          \"id\": \"guitar-section-col\",\n          \"component\": \"Column\",\n          \"children\": [\n            \"guitar-header-row\",\n            \"guitar-tabs\"\n          ]\n        },\n        {\n          \"id\": \"guitar-header-row\",\n          \"component\": \"Row\",\n          \"align\": \"center\",\n          \"children\": [\n            \"guitar-icon\",\n            \"guitar-title\"\n          ]\n        },\n        {\n          \"id\": \"guitar-icon\",\n          \"component\": \"Image\",\n          \"url\": \"/acoustic-guitar.png\",\n          \"variant\": \"icon\"\n        },\n        {\n          \"id\": \"guitar-title\",\n          \"component\": \"Text\",\n          \"text\": \"Guitar Application\",\n          \"variant\": \"h2\"\n        },\n        {\n          \"id\": \"guitar-tabs\",\n          \"component\": \"Tabs\",\n          \"tabs\": [\n            {\n              \"title\": \"Neighborhood Rule\",\n              \"child\": \"rule1-text\"\n            },\n            {\n              \"title\": \"Relative Minors\",\n              \"child\": \"rule2-text\"\n            },\n            {\n              \"title\": \"1-4-5 Progression\",\n              \"child\": \"rule3-text\"\n            }\n          ]\n        },\n        {\n          \"id\": \"rule1-text\",\n          \"component\": \"Text\",\n          \"text\": \"In any given key, the chords immediately to the left and right of your 'home' chord on the circle will always sound good together. For example, if you are in the key of C, the chords F (left) and G (right) are the IV and V chords, the most common companions.\",\n          \"variant\": \"body\"\n        },\n        {\n          \"id\": \"rule2-text\",\n          \"component\": \"Text\",\n          \"text\": \"The inner ring of the circle shows the relative minor for each major key. For C Major, it is A Minor. These two keys share the same notes and can be used interchangeably to create different moods.\",\n          \"variant\": \"body\"\n        },\n        {\n          \"id\": \"rule3-text\",\n          \"component\": \"Text\",\n          \"text\": \"Most rock and blues songs use the I, IV, and V chords. You can find them instantly: Pick a note (I), the one to the left is (IV), and the one to the right is (V).\",\n          \"variant\": \"body\"\n        }\n      ]\n    }\n  }\n]", "raw": {"candidates": [{"content": {"parts": [{"text": "[\n  {\n    \"version\": \"v0.9\",\n    \"createSurface\": {\n      \"surfaceId\": \"music-theory-circle-of-fifths\",\n      \"catalogId\": \"https://genui.local/specification/v0_9/standard_catalog.json\",\n      \"sendDataModel\": false\n    }\n  },\n  {\n    \"version\": \"v0.9\",\n    \"updateComponents\": {\n      \"surfaceId\": \"music-theory-circle-of-fifths\",\n      \"components\": [\n        {\